#!/bin/sh
#
# script/metrics: the project's own numbers, one row per ISO week, and the charts a reader meets
# them in (notes/project-metrics.md).
#
# Why this exists. calef asked for it on 2026-09-02: milestone counts by status over time, decision
# counts by status over time, lines of code and comments over time, coverage over time, and then
# a `BUGS` count, "visible in github and linked from the README". The complaint it answers is
# notes/register-of-measures.md's own opening, one file over: this tree measures a great deal and
# remembers almost none of it. That register says which numbers this kernel owes itself; this
# script is the half that says which ones moved, and when.
#
# **Every row is a RESTATEMENT, not a report.** The series is regenerated from git history by
# applying TODAY's definitions to old commits. That is the right choice for a trend and it is not
# what was reported at the time, and this project has three same-day proofs that the difference is
# real: on 2026-09-02 alone, milestone 214 found 80 sites that printed a skip line and returned
# while counting as passes, milestone 212 found this tree's falsification ratio walking `crates/`
# only, and DECISIONS 139 corrected a figure that conflated a counter's tick resolution with the
# cost of reading it. A dashboard makes numbers look authoritative. notes/project-metrics.md says
# on its face that these are restatements, and this comment is the same warning where the numbers
# are actually produced.
#
# **The naming columns are the sharpest instance of that hazard in this file** (milestone 276). A
# name's provenance lives in the header of the thing it names, and that convention was invented by
# milestone 115 on 2026-08-04. Three weeks of the series predate it, so they restate as a tree full
# of named things and no recorded ratifications, which is a convention's birth rather than a fact
# about the names. `names_total` counts the named things and the four status columns count the ones
# whose block parses, so those weeks show the gap instead of hiding it. notes/project-metrics.md
# says the same thing where the bars are.
#
# What it does NOT build, and why:
#
#   - **Coverage is not re-derived here, and that is a rule about this script rather than about
#     the measurement.** It needs an instrumented build, and nothing here is allowed to build or
#     check anything out (see `--backfill` below), so the column is only ever filled from an lcov
#     file a caller already has. The weekly workflow fills the current week that way. A past week
#     can be filled the same way with `--coverage-for`: check out that week's commit somewhere
#     else, run THAT tree's own `script/coverage` on THAT tree's pinned nightly, and hand this
#     script the lcov it wrote. That is what the live cells measured, so the number means the same
#     thing. notes/project-metrics.md records which weeks were backfilled that way on 2026-09-19,
#     on which toolchains, and the two that could not be measured exactly.
#   - **No plotting dependency.** DECISIONS §46 (thin primitives or whole subsystems, and we
#     write everything in between) makes taking one a decision rather than a shortcut, and a
#     stacked bar is string formatting. The SVGs below are written by hand and a diff can review them.
#
# Usage:
#
#   script/metrics                 print the current commit's row as a table
#   script/metrics --csv           print the current commit's row as one CSV line
#   script/metrics --backfill      rewrite every week's row from git history, then the charts
#   script/metrics --update        recompute THIS week's row in place, then the charts
#   script/metrics --check         non-zero if --update would change anything, a missing week
#                                  included. NOTHING RUNS IT: it cannot gate a pull request,
#                                  because every merge moves the current week's numbers.
#   script/metrics --charts        regenerate the SVGs from the CSV without touching the CSV, and
#                                  rewrite the table of contents on notes/project-metrics.md
#   script/metrics --check-toc     non-zero if that table of contents does not match the page's
#                                  chart headings. `script/lint` runs it; nothing about it moves
#                                  with the week, unlike --check.
#
#   --coverage-min-from <floor.txt>
#                                  fill the CURRENT week's MINIMUM per-file coverage cell from the
#                                  summary `script/coverage` writes beside its lcov
#                                  (target/llvm-cov/floor.txt). Separate from the lcov because the
#                                  minimum is the gate's own view: it is taken over the files the
#                                  floor actually acts on, and the exemptions that decide which
#                                  those are live in `script/coverage` and nowhere else.
#   --coverage-from <lcov.info>    fill the CURRENT week's coverage cell from an lcov file the
#                                  caller produced (`script/coverage` writes
#                                  target/llvm-cov/lcov.info). With --update or --backfill it
#                                  lands on the week HEAD is in and on no other week.
#   script/metrics --coverage-for <WEEK> --coverage-from <lcov.info>
#                                  write that one week's coverage cell and redraw the charts.
#                                  Refuses a week the CSV does not already have, and changes no
#                                  other cell: the row is not recomputed, so its commit and every
#                                  count stay exactly as they were. The lcov must come from that
#                                  week's own commit; nothing here can check that, so say which
#                                  commit and toolchain in the commit message. (Flag name
#                                  provisional, 2026-09-19.)
#
# Name: provisional. Minted 2026-09-02. `metrics` is a noun, which DECISIONS §134 puts on the
# reporting half of the `script/` family (`names`, `citations`, `decisions`, `roadmap`,
# `coverage`, `falsifications` report; `verify`, `test`, `fuzz`, `bench` do something), and this
# only reports. Considered and set aside: `dashboard`, which names the rendering rather than the
# numbers and would go stale the day the charts changed shape; `measures`, which collides with
# notes/register-of-measures.md, a different thing (what the kernel owes itself, rather than what
# moved this week). The directory `notes/project-metrics/` holding the CSV and the SVGs follows
# this name and moves with it. calef has not ratified it.
#
# **One spelling of a week, everywhere.** A week is `2026W36`: the chart axis, the CSV's `week`
# column, this script's own output and the prose on `notes/project-metrics.md` all use it. calef
# ratified the form on 2026-09-02 after seeing three spellings in one page (`2026-W30` in the
# narrative, `202636` on an axis, `2026-W29` in the data), which is two more than a reader should
# have to hold.
#
# It is ISO 8601's designation with the hyphen dropped. It still sorts lexically, it still cannot be
# mistaken for an ordinary number the way a bare `202636` can, and it is the dictionary key that
# makes a rerun replace a row rather than append one.
#
# BUGS
#
# - **`--check` is stale for the length of a commit, by construction.** The current week's row
#   records HEAD's sha in its `commit` column, so committing anything, including the CSV this
#   command just wrote, changes what the next run computes. A working tree that has just committed
#   therefore reports `weeks.csv` stale when it is not. It matters less than it reads: this check is
#   run by the weekly workflow rather than by `script/lint` or CI, so nothing gates on it, and the
#   next scheduled run heals it. Noted 2026-09-20 after a maintainer chased it twice in one session.
# - **Which files carry a name is written twice**: here in `name_surfaces`, and in `script/names`'
#   SURFACES table. The parse is shared (`helpers/name_provenance.py`); the enumeration is not,
#   because this script reads a historical `git ls-tree` and that one reads the working tree. The
#   two drifted once already: milestone 175 moved programs to `components/` and `fixtures/` on
#   2026-09-13, `script/names` was told and this was not, and `names_total` showed a collapse from
#   204 to 133 that never happened until 2026-09-19. Nothing compares the two counts. The cheap
#   check a reader can run is that the current week's `names_total` equals `script/names`' total.
# - **The coverage cell is carried, not recomputed.** A `--backfill` or `--update` keeps whatever
#   coverage a week already had, because it cannot be re-derived without a build. So after the row
#   is repointed at a later commit, the coverage figure describes an earlier commit of that week
#   than every other cell in the row, until someone runs `--coverage-for` against the new commit.
#   2026W38 was in that state from 2026-09-19 until the backfill the same day re-measured it.
# - **Nothing ties an lcov file to a commit.** `--coverage-for` believes the caller about which
#   week the file measured. An lcov produced from the wrong checkout is written without complaint.
# - **Charts are checked by eye, in a browser.** QuickLook crops an SVG's right edge and made the
#   legends look clipped on 2026-09-19 when they were not; render with a browser before believing a
#   layout defect.
# - **The trust-boundary split's crate table is hand-maintained, not derived at every run.** The
#   `unsafe_trust_*` columns (added by the unsafe-census-by-trust-boundary milestone, provisional)
#   classify each `crates/` member as kernel-only, userspace-only, shared or boot-chain, from a
#   `cargo metadata` resolve graph read ONCE on 2026-09-20 and baked into
#   `helpers/rust_source.py` as `KERNEL_ONLY_CRATES`/`USERSPACE_ONLY_CRATES`/`SHARED_CRATES`. It
#   cannot be re-derived per historical week for the same reason the coverage cell cannot: this
#   script is forbidden from running cargo or checking anything out (see the top of this file), so
#   applying today's table to a commit whose dependency graph actually differed is the same
#   restatement trade `MILESTONE_STATUSES`/`NAME_STATUSES` already make. A `crates/` directory the
#   table has never seen counts as `unsafe_trust_unclassified` rather than being silently dropped
#   into the wrong bucket or ignored; a nonzero value there for an OLD week is this bug firing, not
#   a new population appearing. None of the ten weeks on record hit it as of 2026-09-20 (checked by
#   running `--backfill` and reading the column back).
# - **A `SHARED` crate's unsafe is not attributed to a call site.** Whether a particular `unsafe`
#   block in, say, `environment_protocol` is reached by the kernel's production code, by a
#   userspace `std` program, or only by the kernel's own `#[cfg(test)]` test-oracle modules (several
#   of which never ship) is a per-function read this pass did not do. `unsafe_trust_shared` is
#   reported as its own column for exactly this reason: folding it into either side would be a
#   guess wearing a number's clothes. See `notes/project-metrics.md`'s unsafe section.

set -e
cd "$(dirname "$0")/.."

exec python3 - "$@" <<'PYEOF'
import math
import pathlib
import re
import subprocess
import sys
from datetime import date, datetime, timedelta, timezone

# The derivations this script shares with `script/lint`, in one place rather than copied into both
# (milestone 236: three derivations are copied between scripts, and nothing notices when they
# drift). The import works because this script `cd`s to the repository root before it runs python,
# which is what made a shared module possible without turning a `#!/bin/sh` entry point into the
# consumer of a built artifact. What stays here rather than moving is the file SOURCING below: this
# script reads blobs at historical revisions and never checks anything out, and that tolerance is
# deliberately not in the shared count.
# `dont_write_bytecode` because the import would otherwise leave a `helpers/__pycache__/`
# behind on every run, which is an untracked directory a gate has no business creating.
sys.dont_write_bytecode = True
sys.path.insert(0, 'helpers')
import baseline_drift                                                       # noqa: E402
import interface_stability                                                  # noqa: E402
import name_provenance                                                      # noqa: E402
import prose_ratchet                                                        # noqa: E402
import roadmap_block                                                        # noqa: E402
import roadmap_proposals                                                    # noqa: E402
import rust_source                                                          # noqa: E402
from rust_source import non_blank, strip_non_code                           # noqa: E402

PAGE = 'notes/project-metrics.md'
# The page's table of contents is written by this script between these two lines and nowhere else
# (calef, 2026-09-24). A hand-kept one drifts the first time a panel is added, and panels are added
# weekly; `script/lint` runs `--check-toc`, so a stale one fails a build rather than a reader.
TOC_BEGIN = '<!-- toc: script/metrics writes this -->'
TOC_END = '<!-- /toc -->'
DATA_DIR = pathlib.Path('notes/project-metrics')
# The data files themselves are one per measure and are named by `MEASURES` below. There is no
# single joined file; milestone 581 (one metrics file per measure) retired `weekly.csv`.
# Milestone 519 (what this project costs, tracked where it cannot rot) needs two inputs that no
# revision of this tree can produce. `effort.csv` is written by
# `script/effort` from session records that exist only on the machine the lanes ran on; `ledger.md`
# is typed by a person. Both are read from the WORKING TREE for every week, never from a week's own
# revision, for `built_by_week`'s reason one level over: they are records of a fact rather than
# snapshots of a tree, and reading them late is not a revision of history.
EFFORT_PATH = DATA_DIR / 'effort.csv'
LEDGER_PATH = DATA_DIR / 'ledger.md'

# **This list is the roadmap's status vocabulary and it has to stay all of it**, because a status
# missing here is counted as nothing: `milestones()` below keys on this dict and skips a row whose
# token it does not hold, so `milestones_total` goes short by exactly the milestones it cannot see.
# That is not hypothetical. `SUPERSEDED` was minted 2026-09-15 and never added, so seven blocks were
# invisible in every weekly row until milestone 448 (a refusal gets a number, a status, and a
# condition that would change it) added both it and `REFUSED`. A backfill restates the history, so
# the correction reaches the weeks that were already written rather than only the next one.
MILESTONE_STATUSES = ['BUILT', 'PARTIAL', 'IN-PROGRESS', 'REMOVED',
                      'NOT-STARTED', 'OPTIONAL', 'RECORDED', 'SUPERSEDED', 'REFUSED']
DECISION_STATUSES = ['DECIDED', 'AMENDED', 'SUPERSEDED', 'PROPOSED', 'UNRECORDED']
# The naming vocabulary is `script/names`', taken from the shared module rather than retyped, so a
# fifth word added there arrives here as a column instead of as a silent undercount. It is lower
# case where the other two are upper, because these are the words a `Name:` block literally spells.
NAME_STATUSES = name_provenance.STATUSES

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Which model wrote it (the commits-and-lines-by-model milestone, provisional).
#
# **The source is the `Co-Authored-By` trailer, and the richer source was refused on purpose.** The
# agent harness's own session records carry tokens per model, which is a better measure of effort
# than either of the two columns below. They are not usable here and the reasons are structural
# rather than a matter of taste: they are not in git, they live on one laptop and nothing promises
# to keep them (`script/effort`'s header already records 2026W29 through 2026W33 as unrecoverable);
# they cannot be backfilled, and git can; they see one machine, which is not hypothetical, because
# `Claude Fable 5` signs 360 commits here and appears in NO session record on the machine those
# records live on; and this script may not read anything but git, by the rule at the top of this
# file, because `.github/workflows/metrics.yml` runs it on a GitHub runner where none of that
# exists. A trailer-based series can live here. A token-based one cannot.
#
# **Every commit falls in exactly one of three buckets, and the sum is checked** (calef,
# 2026-09-24). `attributed` (a trailer, counted per model), `unattributed` (authored, no trailer),
# and `merge`. `models_by_week` asserts that the three add up to the week's whole commit count, and
# a mismatch is a hard failure rather than a note. That assertion is the point of the shape: every
# other miscount this tree has written up was a count over a set nobody could see, and this one is
# auditable by arithmetic. If normalization ever drops a name, or a rule is wrong, the total stops
# balancing and something says so.
#
# **Normalization collapses a variant, never a version.** `Claude Opus 5 (1M context)` and
# `Claude Opus 5` are one model addressed through two context windows, so the trailing parenthetical
# is stripped. `Claude Fable 5.1` keeps its own column, because a version is a different model and
# folding it into `Claude Fable 5` would be exactly the silent merge this table exists to prevent.
#
# **A name this table has never seen is counted in `other_models` AND printed by name on every
# run.** It is not dropped and it does not quietly become an existing model. That is this tree's
# recurring "checked nothing, found nothing" failure (MILESTONE_STATUSES above carries the same
# scar: a status missing from a list was counted as zero for five days), and the difference here is
# that the bucket is visible in the CSV, the value is named on stderr, and the invariant still
# balances, so the reader is told rather than reassured.
MODEL_SLUGS = ['opus_5', 'opus_5_5', 'opus_4_8', 'sonnet_5', 'fable_5', 'fable_5_1', 'other_models']

# Every trailer value this repository has ever carried, mapped to its column. Checked 2026-09-24
# against all 5,487 commits: 2,934 carry a trailer and these seven spellings are all of them.
# `Claude Opus 5.5` joined the same day, when the tree switched to it: until it had a row here every
# commit it signed fell into `other_models`, which is the warning below doing its job.
# `Claude Code` is the tool rather than a model; it signs exactly one commit, from before the
# convention settled, and it is recorded here so that it lands in `other_models` as a decision
# rather than as an unrecognized value warned about on every run for the rest of the project.
MODEL_TRAILERS = {
    'Claude Opus 5': 'opus_5',
    'Claude Opus 5.5': 'opus_5_5',
    'Claude Opus 4.8': 'opus_4_8',
    'Claude Sonnet 5': 'sonnet_5',
    'Claude Fable 5': 'fable_5',
    'Claude Fable 5.1': 'fable_5_1',
    'Claude Code': 'other_models',
}
MODEL_UNKNOWN = 'other_models'

# The three buckets no model owns. `pre_convention` is split out of `unattributed` because the two
# hide different facts: a commit made before the first trailer ever appeared could not have carried
# one, and a commit made after it and carrying none is a gap. Only the second is a defect.
MODEL_EXTRA = ['unattributed', 'pre_convention', 'merge']


def _model_col(kind, slug):
    return 'model_' + kind + '_' + slug


def normalize_model(raw):
    """A trailer value, reduced to the model it names. See MODEL_TRAILERS above."""
    name = raw.split('<')[0].strip()
    return re.sub(r'\s*\([^)]*\)\s*$', '', name).strip()



def _col(prefix, status):
    return prefix + '_' + status.lower().replace('-', '_')


# One file per measure, which is what makes a new measure a new file rather than a new column
# (milestone 581, one metrics file per measure). `weekly.csv` held all of these side by side and
# was 58 columns wide, so every branch that added a measure rewrote the same header line and the
# same eleven data lines. Two pull requests adding disjoint measures collided with each other and
# with `main`; two of them aborted a rebase in one batch on 2026-09-23. A measure in its own file
# cannot collide with a measure in another one, which is AGENTS.md's top rung: the wrong state is
# unrepresentable rather than merely discouraged.
#
# The boundaries are the charts'. `notes/project-metrics.md` already presents these as separate
# panels, so a reader who knows the page knows the files, and `CHARTS` below is the index that
# names both. `weeks` is the one entry that is not a measure: it is the series index, recording
# which commit and date each week's numbers were read at.
#
# Two consequences worth knowing before reading `read_csv` and `render_csvs`. A row is written only
# when its measure has something to say for that week, so a series that began in 2026W39 starts
# there instead of carrying ten blank cells. And `read_csv` merges every file back into one row per
# week, so everything downstream of it, the charts included, still sees the flat row it always saw.
#
# `cost` rather than `effort`, because `effort.csv` in this same directory is `script/effort`'s
# output and an input to this script. The chart it feeds keeps its own name, `effort.svg`.
WEEK = 'week'

MEASURES = [
    ('weeks', ['commit', 'date']),
    ('milestones', [_col('milestones', s) for s in MILESTONE_STATUSES]
     + ['milestones_total', 'proposals_unnumbered']),
    ('velocity', ['milestones_built_this_week']),
    ('pull-requests', ['merged_pull_requests']),
    # The model-attribution columns (file name `models.csv`; Name: ratified 2026-09-24 (calef, in session, when asked to ratify the two metrics files the split produced; nothing refused)). Commits
    # and lines are one measure and one file rather than two, because a single pass over the same
    # git log produces both and a week that has one always has the other. The two charts it feeds
    # keep their own names, `models-commits.svg` and `models-lines.svg`, the way `cost` feeds
    # `effort.svg`.
    ('models', [_model_col('commits', s) for s in MODEL_SLUGS + MODEL_EXTRA]
     + ['model_commits_total']
     + [_model_col('lines', s) for s in MODEL_SLUGS + ['unattributed', 'pre_convention']]),
    ('decisions', [_col('decisions', s) for s in DECISION_STATUSES] + ['decisions_total']),
    ('names', [_col('names', s) for s in NAME_STATUSES] + ['names_total']),
    ('lines', ['kernel_code_lines', 'kernel_comment_lines',
               'other_code_lines', 'other_comment_lines']),
    ('bugs', ['bugs_markdown', 'bugs_rust']),
    ('harnesses', ['harnesses_falsified', 'harnesses_unfalsified', 'harnesses_total']),
    ('unsafe', ['unsafe_outside_arch', 'unsafe_inside_arch', 'unsafe_thread_safety',
                'unsafe_code_lines', 'unsafe_density']),
    ('unsafe-trust', ['unsafe_trust_kernel', 'unsafe_trust_kernel_code_lines',
                      'unsafe_trust_kernel_density',
                      'unsafe_trust_userspace', 'unsafe_trust_userspace_code_lines',
                      'unsafe_trust_userspace_density',
                      'unsafe_trust_shared', 'unsafe_trust_boot_chain',
                      'unsafe_trust_unclassified']),
    ('fatal-risks', ['fatal_risks_run', 'fatal_risks_not_run', 'fatal_risks_cannot_run',
                     'fatal_risks_total']),
    ('coverage', ['coverage_lines_pct', 'coverage_min_file_pct']),
    ('prose-budget', ['prose_excess_words', 'prose_documents_over_cap']),
    # Temporary, and it says so where it is drawn: see `bold_backlog` and BOLD_RETIRE below. The
    # file name and both column names are provisional (lane/faux-headings, 2026-09-26).
    ('bold-backlog', ['bold_excess_spans', 'bold_documents_over']),
    # Milestone 415 (sub-tripwire drift accumulates across baseline saves), item 3: icount drift
    # since a fixed anchor. calef ruled on 2026-09-26 that it is a report and not a gate, in §190
    # (must an icount baseline save record why it moved). See DRIFT_BEGIN below. File and column names provisional (that lane's).
    ('baseline-drift', [prefix + arch for arch in baseline_drift.ARCHES
                        for prefix in ('baseline_drift_max_pct_', 'baseline_drift_rows_over_5pct_')]),
    # Milestone 610 (provisional): the interface's stability. `helpers/interface_stability.py`
    # writes these cells, because they need `cargo doc`, which nothing in this script may run; this
    # script only carries them (INTERFACE_FIELDS below) and draws them. File and column names
    # provisional (that lane's, 2026-09-26).
    ('interface-stability', interface_stability.FIELDS),
    # Milestone 519's cost columns. Three units on purpose, and no fourth column adding them up:
    # person-weeks for the comparison against the verification literature, tokens with a dated
    # price for the argument about the method, cash for calef's own budgeting. 519 refuses a single
    # headline number, so this file does not compute one.
    ('cost', ['human_person_weeks', 'lane_tokens', 'lane_wall_clock_hours',
              'price_per_mtok_at_date', 'cash_spend']),
    # What a turn costs, from the same committed `effort.csv` as `cost` above (file name
    # `context-per-turn.csv`, after the chart it feeds; Name: ratified 2026-09-24 (calef, in
    # session, when asked to ratify the two metrics files the split produced; nothing refused)).
    # These answer a different question from `lane_tokens`: not how much was spent, but what the
    # unit of spending is. A turn is billed for the context it carried, and these say how large that
    # was. Flows and a peak, never a stock: `lane_turns` and the share are per-week events, and
    # `lane_context_per_turn_peak` is the largest single request in the week. The stock-versus-flow
    # line is the one this file already draws for `built_by_week`, and it is drawn again here
    # because this data is exactly the shape that invites the mistake.
    ('context-per-turn', ['lane_turns', 'lane_cache_read_share_pct',
                          'lane_context_per_turn_mean', 'lane_context_per_turn_peak']),
]

# The flat row every metric function and `--table` still speaks in, derived from the split rather
# than maintained beside it, so the two cannot disagree about which columns exist.
FIELDS = [WEEK] + [column for _name, columns in MEASURES for column in columns]
if len(set(FIELDS)) != len(FIELDS):
    raise SystemExit('metrics: MEASURES names a column twice; a column belongs to one measure')


def measure_path(name):
    return DATA_DIR / (name + '.csv')

# Columns a rewrite must CARRY rather than recompute, because nothing in git can produce them and
# the only copy is the cell itself. `--backfill` drops every row and rebuilds it, so a column not
# named here is silently erased by one. That is not hypothetical: milestone 276 ran a backfill to
# add a column and watched 2026W36's measured 94.4 coverage disappear with it, which is why the
# mechanism is now a named set instead of one special case in `main`. It was found by
# milestone 276 (the dashboard counts milestones and decisions by status, and names not at all).
#
# `human_person_weeks` joins coverage here for a different reason than coverage's. It is DERIVED on
# a new row, at 1.0 per calendar week, from calef's standing datum that he works on this full time
# (milestone 519, which refuses time tracking and asks only for a correction when the datum stops
# being true). Carrying it is what makes that correction survive: a hand-edited 0.4 for a week he
# was away stays 0.4 through every later backfill, and nothing has to remember it was edited.
CARRIED_FIELDS = ('coverage_lines_pct', 'coverage_min_file_pct', 'human_person_weeks')

# Milestone 610's columns are carried for coverage's reason: they come from a build this script is
# not allowed to run, so the only copy is the cell `helpers/interface_stability.py` wrote. A
# `--backfill` here would otherwise erase the series it cannot restate.
INTERFACE_FIELDS = tuple(interface_stability.FIELDS)
CARRIED_FIELDS = CARRIED_FIELDS + INTERFACE_FIELDS

# calef's standing datum, applied to a week that has no cell yet. See CARRIED_FIELDS.
DEFAULT_PERSON_WEEKS = '1.0'


def git(*args):
    return subprocess.run(['git', *args], capture_output=True, check=True).stdout


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Reading a commit's tree without touching the working tree.
#
# `git show <rev>:<path>` per file would be several thousand processes over nine weeks. One
# `git ls-tree -r -z` names the blobs and one `git cat-file --batch` streams every one of them, so
# a whole week's tree costs two processes. Nothing is ever checked out: AGENTS.md's rule against
# `git checkout <path>` exists because it destroyed uncommitted work three times, and a backfill
# that walks history is exactly where that mistake would be made.

# What a week's tree has to carry. `.rs` and `.md` are the line, comment, `BUGS`, harness and
# `unsafe` counts. `Cargo.toml` and `script/` joined them for the naming columns (milestone 276):
# a package's provenance block lives in its manifest, because a package here may have a `lib.rs`, a
# `main.rs` or neither, and a `script/` entry point's lives in its shell comment header. Widening
# the filter costs about sixty more blobs in the same single `cat-file --batch`, and changes nothing
# else, because every other metric selects by suffix out of the same dictionary.
def _wanted(path):
    if path.startswith(('vendor/', 'target/')):
        return False
    return (path.endswith(('.rs', '.md', '/Cargo.toml'))
            or (path.startswith('script/') and path.count('/') == 1))


class Tree:
    """Every blob a metric reads in one commit: Rust, markdown, manifests and `script/` entries."""

    def __init__(self, rev):
        self.rev = rev
        paths, oids = [], []
        for entry in git('ls-tree', '-r', '-z', rev).decode('utf-8', 'replace').split('\0'):
            if not entry:
                continue
            meta, path = entry.split('\t', 1)
            _mode, kind, oid = meta.split()
            if kind != 'blob' or not _wanted(path):
                continue
            paths.append(path)
            oids.append(oid)
        self.text = {}
        if not oids:
            return
        blob = subprocess.run(['git', 'cat-file', '--batch'],
                              input=('\n'.join(oids) + '\n').encode(),
                              capture_output=True, check=True).stdout
        pos = 0
        for path in paths:
            end = blob.index(b'\n', pos)
            size = int(blob[pos:end].split()[2])
            self.text[path] = blob[end + 1:end + 1 + size].decode('utf-8', 'replace')
            pos = end + 1 + size + 1

    def rust(self):
        return [(p, t) for p, t in self.text.items() if p.endswith('.rs')]

    def markdown(self):
        return [(p, t) for p, t in self.text.items() if p.endswith('.md')]


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The metrics. Each one takes a Tree and returns numbers; none of them builds anything.

# The roadmap index row: `| 231 | NOT-STARTED | [title](file) | note | date |`, read ONLY from the
# single `design/roadmap.md` that trees before milestone 76 (split the roadmap: one file per
# milestone) carried. The status vocabulary is checked rather than assumed, because that file also
# held a vocabulary table whose rows start with a pipe. Every tree with blocks is counted from the
# blocks; see `_blocks` below.
# The statuses whose index row carries a date, which is `script/roadmap`'s own `DATED`. Both mean
# the milestone turned BUILT on that day; REMOVED keeps the date because removal does not unbuild
# it.
DATED_STATUSES = ('BUILT', 'REMOVED')

# A bare `YYYY-MM-DD` anywhere in the Built cell, which is more forgiving than anchoring the cell:
# a handful of early rows carry a parenthetical beside the date.
_DATE = re.compile(r'\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2}')

_INDEX_ROW = re.compile(r'^\|\s*(\d+)\s*\|\s*([A-Z][A-Z0-9 _-]*?)\s*\|')

# The blocks, for every revision since milestone 76 split the roadmap. Each block states its own
# number (the filename), its own status (the `**Status:` line) and, since milestone 294, its own
# Built date (the `**Built:**` line under `## Index row`), so the fields these two metrics want are
# in the tree one file per milestone.
#
# **`design/roadmap/README.md` is never read** (calef, 2026-09-24: *"There need not be a check the
# file for milestone status at all because it cannot be found there any longer."*). Until that day
# this preferred the README's index table wherever one existed, on the argument that a past week
# should be counted the way its reader counted it. Measured when the preference was removed, it
# bought nothing: a backfill from the blocks alone reproduced every `milestones.csv` and
# `velocity.csv` row from 2026W29 to 2026W37 exactly. The one historical difference is 2026W38,
# where milestone 518 (a census that cannot be attributed is a number nobody can act on) says
# `BUILT 2026-09-20` in its block and had no index row at the week's end, so the index was stale
# and the block was right. And the preference had a live cost: when #1247 brought the README back
# as a pointer, both readers took it for an empty index and zeroed the series.
_BLOCK = re.compile(r'^design/roadmap/(\d+)-[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]*\.md$')


def _blocks(tree):
    """(number, status, built) per milestone block in one tree."""
    out = []
    for path, text in tree.text.items():
        m = _BLOCK.match(path)
        if not m:
            continue
        # Either form: this walks revisions on both sides of the switch in milestone 596 (the roadmap blocks
        # get frontmatter too).
        status, built = roadmap_block.status_and_built(text)
        if not status:
            continue
        out.append((m.group(1), status, built))
    return out


def _pre_split_roadmap(tree):
    """The single `design/roadmap.md` of a tree from before milestone 76, or None for any tree
    that has per-milestone blocks, which is then counted from them."""
    if any(_BLOCK.match(path) for path in tree.text):
        return None
    return tree.text.get('design/roadmap.md')


def milestones(tree):
    counts = dict.fromkeys(MILESTONE_STATUSES, 0)
    text = _pre_split_roadmap(tree)
    if not text:
        for _number, status, _built in _blocks(tree):
            if status in counts:
                counts[status] += 1
        return counts
    seen = set()
    for line in text.split('\n'):
        match = _INDEX_ROW.match(line)
        if not match:
            continue
        number, status = match.group(1), match.group(2)
        if status in counts and number not in seen:
            seen.add(number)
            counts[status] += 1
    return counts


def built_by_week(tree):
    """How many milestones turned BUILT in each ISO week, from one tree, keyed by week.

    **A flow, where every other milestone column here is a stock**, and the only one that answers
    "how fast". That difference is why it is derived differently, and the reason is measured rather
    than assumed.

    **It is read from the CURRENT tree for every week, not from each week's own revision**, which
    breaks this file's usual rule on purpose. Computing it per revision was tried first and is
    wrong: the index milestone 294 (`design/roadmap/README.md`'s index is generated) generated
    was regenerated at an integrator's convenience while it existed, so a week whose revision predates its own
    regeneration reports zero and the next week reports both. Backfilled that way on 2026-09-20 it
    gave 0 for 2026W29 through 2026W31 and 0 for 2026W33, against 10, 8, 20 and 9 actually built in
    those weeks. A stock read late is merely stale; a flow read late lands in the wrong bucket,
    which is worse than stale because it looks like history.

    A BUILT date is a stable fact (`script/roadmap`: a milestone built on a date turned BUILT on
    that date, and nothing later makes that untrue), so reading them all from today's tree is not a
    revision of history, it is the only way to get it right. The cost is that this column, alone
    among these, changes for a past week if somebody corrects that week's date, and correcting it
    is what we would want.

    REMOVED rows keep their date and are counted here: a week in which something was built and then
    removed still had the building in it.
    """
    text = _pre_split_roadmap(tree)
    counts = {}
    if not text:
        for _number, status, built in _blocks(tree):
            if status in DATED_STATUSES and built:
                key = week_of_date(built)
                counts[key] = counts.get(key, 0) + 1
        return counts
    seen = set()
    for line in text.split('\n'):
        match = _INDEX_ROW.match(line)
        if not match:
            continue
        cells = [c.strip() for c in line.split('|')]
        if len(cells) < 6:
            continue
        number, status = match.group(1), match.group(2)
        if number in seen or status not in DATED_STATUSES:
            continue
        seen.add(number)
        stamp = _DATE.search(cells[5])
        if stamp:
            key = week_of_date(stamp.group(0))
            counts[key] = counts.get(key, 0) + 1
    return counts


def merges_by_week(rev):
    """How many pull requests merged in each ISO week, from git alone, keyed by week.

    **A flow beside `built_by_week`, and the pairing is the point.** Milestones count DECLARED units
    of work, and a large share of what lands here is not one: a decision, a correction, a record
    fix, a capture. On 2026-09-21 the maintainer opened about fifteen pull requests of which three
    were milestones, and every column on this page was blind to the other twelve. That work is real
    and it costs real tokens, which is the reason calef asked for this column in milestone 519's
    lane rather than a later one.

    **It is derived from git and never from the GitHub API**, which is what makes it backfill to the
    first commit like everything else here. A merge commit carries its own committer date and
    `main` holds every one of them; Actions' run history is retained for ninety days and would have
    given a series that started in June and could never be extended backwards.

    Read from HEAD for every week rather than from each week's own revision, for the same reason
    `built_by_week` is: a merge's date is a fact that does not change, so one pass over one log
    answers every week at once, and a past week's answer is identical either way.

    The shape matched is GitHub's own default merge-commit subject, `Merge pull request #N from ...`,
    which is what the merge queue writes. A merge made any other way is not counted, and there is no
    way to tell one from an ordinary merge commit after the fact.
    """
    counts = {}
    log = git('log', '--first-parent', '--format=%cI%x09%s', rev).decode('utf-8', 'replace')
    for entry in log.split('\n'):
        if '\t' not in entry:
            continue
        when, subject = entry.split('\t', 1)
        if not subject.startswith('Merge pull request #'):
            continue
        key = week_of(when)
        counts[key] = counts.get(key, 0) + 1
    return counts


def models_by_week(rev):
    """Commits and lines by the model that signed them, per ISO week, from git alone.

    **The third flow on this page, and it answers a question the other two cannot**: the velocity
    and pull-request columns say how much landed, and this says who wrote it. The source is the
    `Co-Authored-By` trailer `AGENTS.md` has every agent commit carry; MODEL_TRAILERS above has the
    argument for why the harness's own token records were refused as the spine.

    Read from HEAD for every week rather than from each week's own revision, exactly like
    `merges_by_week`: a commit's trailer and its diff are facts that do not change, so one pass
    over one log answers every week at once.

    **Three buckets, mutually exclusive, and the sum is asserted.**

    - `merge`, first, because a merge commit is not authored work. On this repository
      `allow_squash_merge` is false and the merge queue creates them, so a merge commit IS a pull
      request landing, which makes this a throughput count worth having on its own terms rather
      than a residue. 95 merge commits do carry a trailer, from an agent resolving a conflict, and
      the bucket still wins: the commit records a landing, not a piece of writing.
    - `attributed`, per model, for a non-merge commit whose trailer this table knows.
    - `unattributed`, for a non-merge commit with no trailer, split into `pre_convention` (before
      the first trailer this repository ever carried) and `unattributed` proper (after it, which is
      the half that is actually a gap).

    **Merges carry a commit count and no line count**, and the reason is that a merge's diff
    against its first parent is the whole of what landed on the branch, every line of which is
    already counted on the branch's own commits. Adding it would double the tree.

    **Lines are `added + removed`, the churn, not the net.** Net is the wrong quantity twice over:
    a commit that only deletes reads negative, and a careful mechanical sweep across 246 files can
    net out near zero while being the largest diff of the week. The tree's net size already has a
    series of its own (`lines.svg`), which is a stock; this is a flow, and what flows is how much
    text was touched.

    A week entirely before the convention gets EMPTY cells for every model column and for
    `unattributed`, not zeroes, which is the same absent-is-not-zero rule milestone 519's cost
    columns follow. `pre_convention`, `merge` and `model_commits_total` are still filled for such a
    week, because those three are countable without any trailer existing.
    """
    slugs = MODEL_SLUGS + MODEL_EXTRA
    weeks = {}
    unknown = {}

    def bucket(week):
        return weeks.setdefault(week, {s: 0 for s in slugs})

    # One `git log --numstat` over the whole history: two numbers and a trailer per commit, in one
    # process, the same economy `Tree` buys with one `cat-file --batch`. RS separates the records
    # because a commit subject can hold anything, US separates trailers because a trailer value can
    # hold a comma.
    fmt = '%x1e%H%x09%cI%x09%(trailers:key=Co-Authored-By,valueonly,separator=%x1f)'
    log = git('log', '--numstat', '--format=' + fmt, rev).decode('utf-8', 'replace')
    merge_parents = set(git('log', '--min-parents=2', '--format=%H', rev).decode().split())
    first_trailer = None
    records = []
    for chunk in log.split('\x1e'):
        if not chunk.strip():
            continue
        head, _, rest = chunk.partition('\n')
        sha, when, trailers = head.split('\t')
        names = [normalize_model(t) for t in trailers.split('\x1f') if t.strip()]
        churn = 0
        for line in rest.split('\n'):
            if not line.strip():
                continue
            added, removed, _ = line.split('\t', 2)
            churn += (int(added) if added != '-' else 0) + (int(removed) if removed != '-' else 0)
        records.append((sha, when, names, churn))
        if names and sha not in merge_parents and (first_trailer is None or when < first_trailer):
            first_trailer = when

    for sha, when, names, churn in records:
        week = week_of(when)
        row = bucket(week)
        row['_commits'] = row.get('_commits', 0) + 1
        if sha in merge_parents:
            row['merge'] += 1
            continue
        if names:
            # **A commit with more than one trailer is counted once, under the first, and named on
            # stderr.** Counting it twice would break the invariant this function asserts, and
            # splitting a commit in half would put a fraction in a column of counts. Measured
            # 2026-09-24: no commit in this repository carries two, so the rule has never fired and
            # the warning is what would tell us it started to.
            if len(names) > 1:
                unknown.setdefault('(%d trailers on one commit) %s' % (len(names), sha), 0)
                unknown['(%d trailers on one commit) %s' % (len(names), sha)] += 1
            slug = MODEL_TRAILERS.get(names[0])
            if slug is None:
                unknown[names[0]] = unknown.get(names[0], 0) + 1
                slug = MODEL_UNKNOWN
            row[slug] += 1
            row[_model_col('lines', slug)] = row.get(_model_col('lines', slug), 0) + churn
        elif first_trailer is not None and when < first_trailer:
            row['pre_convention'] += 1
            row['model_lines_pre_convention'] = row.get('model_lines_pre_convention', 0) + churn
        else:
            row['unattributed'] += 1
            row['model_lines_unattributed'] = row.get('model_lines_unattributed', 0) + churn

    if unknown:
        # Named, never swallowed. See MODEL_TRAILERS.
        print('metrics: %d commit trailer value(s) this script has never seen, counted in '
              'other_models: %s' % (sum(unknown.values()),
                                    '; '.join('%s (%d)' % (k, v) for k, v in sorted(unknown.items()))),
              file=sys.stderr)

    out = {}
    for week, row in weeks.items():
        total = row.pop('_commits')
        counted = sum(row[s] for s in slugs)
        if counted != total:
            # Loud, because a denominator that leaks quietly is the failure this whole shape was
            # built to make impossible. There is no softer self-check in this script to follow.
            raise SystemExit('metrics: %s does not balance: %d commits, %d bucketed '
                             '(attributed + unattributed + merge must be every commit)'
                             % (week, total, counted))
        cells = {_model_col('commits', s): row[s] for s in slugs}
        cells['model_commits_total'] = total
        for s in MODEL_SLUGS + ['unattributed', 'pre_convention']:
            cells[_model_col('lines', s)] = row.get(_model_col('lines', s), 0)
        # A week wholly before the first trailer could not have carried one. Absent, not zero.
        if first_trailer is not None and week < week_of(first_trailer):
            for s in MODEL_SLUGS + ['unattributed']:
                cells[_model_col('commits', s)] = ''
                cells[_model_col('lines', s)] = ''
        out[week] = cells
    return out


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Milestone 519's cost inputs, which are the two things on this page that no revision can produce.
#
# `notes/project-metrics/effort.csv` is written by `script/effort` from the agent harness's session
# records, which live on one laptop outside this repository and are discarded. `ledger.md` is typed
# by a person. Both are committed, so the weekly workflow on a GitHub runner reads exactly what a
# run on the dev Mac reads, and produces the same bytes.
#
# **An absent week is absent, never zero.** The first five weeks of this project have no session
# records left anywhere, and a zero in a cost column would be averaged into a published figure by
# somebody who never read this comment. Every function below returns nothing for a week it cannot
# answer for, and every cell it does not fill stays empty.

# `| 2026-08-15 | argon, ... | 89.99 | source |` and the two other table shapes in the
# ledger. Parsed by the heading they sit under, because three tables in one file cannot be told
# apart by their columns alone and guessing from column count is how a ledger silently loses a row.
_LEDGER_DATE = re.compile(r'^\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2}$')


def _money(cell):
    """A price cell, or None. Commas and a leading currency symbol are tolerated, prose is not."""
    text = cell.replace(',', '').replace('$', '').strip()
    text = re.sub(r'\*+', '', text)
    try:
        return float(text)
    except ValueError:
        return None


def ledger():
    """What was paid and what a token lists for, out of `notes/project-metrics/ledger.md`.

    Returns (purchases, subscriptions, rates):
      purchases     [(YYYY-MM-DD, usd)]
      subscriptions [(YYYY-MM-DD start, usd per month)]
      rates         {model: {'input': usd, 'cache_write_5m': ..., ...}} per million tokens

    A missing file is not an error: it means the cost columns stay empty, which is the correct
    reading of a tree that has not recorded a cost yet.
    """
    purchases, subscriptions, rates = [], [], {}
    if not LEDGER_PATH.exists():
        return purchases, subscriptions, rates
    section = ''
    for line in LEDGER_PATH.read_text().split('\n'):
        if line.startswith('#'):
            section = line.lstrip('#').strip().lower()
            continue
        if not line.startswith('|'):
            continue
        cells = [c.strip() for c in line.strip().strip('|').split('|')]
        if section == 'hardware' and len(cells) >= 3 and _LEDGER_DATE.match(cells[0]):
            price = _money(cells[2])
            if price is not None:
                purchases.append((cells[0], price))
        elif section == 'subscriptions' and len(cells) >= 4 and _LEDGER_DATE.match(cells[0]):
            # `200.00 / month`, which is the only cadence this tree has ever had. A rate written in
            # any other unit is skipped rather than guessed at, and the ledger's own BUGS says so.
            rate = _money(cells[3].split('/')[0]) if '/ month' in cells[3] else None
            if rate is not None:
                subscriptions.append((cells[0], rate))
        elif (section.startswith('retail rates') and len(cells) >= 7
              and _LEDGER_DATE.match(cells[0])):
            model = cells[1].strip('`')
            values = [_money(c) for c in cells[2:7]]
            if model and all(v is not None for v in values):
                # A later row for the same model replaces an earlier one: the ledger is appended to,
                # so the last row wins, and the file's own BUGS records that this restates old weeks
                # at the newest rate.
                rates[model] = dict(zip(('input', 'cache_write_5m', 'cache_write_1h',
                                         'cache_read', 'output'), values))
    return purchases, subscriptions, rates


def _subscription_charges(subscriptions, through):
    """[(YYYY-MM-DD, usd)] for every monthly charge from each start date up to `through`.

    The billing day is the monthly anniversary of the start date, which is an assumption the ledger
    states out loud because no invoice is recorded anywhere. A month with no such day (a start on
    the 31st) bills on its last day.
    """
    out = []
    for start, rate in subscriptions:
        first = date.fromisoformat(start)
        year, month = first.year, first.month
        while True:
            if month == 12:
                last = 31
            else:
                last = (date(year + (month == 12), month % 12 + 1, 1)
                        - timedelta(days=1)).day
            when = date(year, month, min(first.day, last))
            if when > through:
                break
            if when >= first:
                out.append((when.isoformat(), rate))
            month += 1
            if month > 12:
                year, month = year + 1, 1
    return out


def cash_by_week(through):
    """{ISO week: dollars actually paid that week}, from the ledger.

    **Paid, not accrued**, which is milestone 519's own definition of the column and is why it is
    lumpy: three weeks carry a subscription charge and seven carry nothing. An accrual is derivable
    from the ledger by anyone who wants one; a payment is not derivable from an accrual, so the
    file keeps the one that cannot be reconstructed.
    """
    purchases, subscriptions, _rates = ledger()
    out = {}
    for when, amount in purchases + _subscription_charges(subscriptions, through):
        key = week_of_date(when)
        out[key] = out.get(key, 0.0) + amount
    return out


def effort_by_week():
    """{ISO week: dict of the cost and context figures} from `effort.csv`.

    `tokens` is every token class added together, which is the physical quantity and is the only
    thing on this page a reader can compare against another project's. The price is kept separate
    rather than folded in, because the four classes are billed between 0.1x and 2x of each other and
    one number would hide which.

    **The blended rate is left blank for a week whose mix this tree cannot price**, rather than
    priced over the part it can. A rate derived from three quarters of a week's tokens is not that
    week's rate, and a number that looks like a measurement is the one thing this page cannot
    afford to be casual with.

    **The context figures ride along because they come out of the same rows**, and they answer the
    other half of the cost question. `turns` is the request count. `read_share` is the cache-read
    share of every token spent, which is the number that reframes the question: it is near 98% and
    that is not waste, it is what carrying a long session's context costs. `context_mean` is the
    whole prompt (input plus cache write plus cache read) over the turns that carried it, and
    `context_peak` is the largest single request, which is a MAXIMUM over models rather than a sum
    for the reason `script/effort`'s `MAXED` gives: adding two peaks invents a request nobody made.
    """
    if not EFFORT_PATH.exists():
        return {}
    _p, _s, rates = ledger()
    text = EFFORT_PATH.read_text().strip().split('\n')
    header = text[0].split(',')
    weeks = {}
    for line in text[1:]:
        if not line.strip():
            continue
        row = dict(zip(header, line.split(',')))
        week = row['week']
        held = weeks.setdefault(week, {'tokens': 0, 'hours': 0.0, 'usd': 0.0, 'priced': True,
                                       'turns': 0, 'context': 0, 'read': 0, 'peak': 0})
        counts = {
            'input': int(row.get('input_tokens') or 0),
            'cache_write_5m': int(row.get('cache_write_5m_tokens') or 0),
            'cache_write_1h': int(row.get('cache_write_1h_tokens') or 0),
            'cache_read': int(row.get('cache_read_tokens') or 0),
            'output': int(row.get('output_tokens') or 0),
        }
        total = sum(counts.values())
        held['tokens'] += total
        held['hours'] += float(row.get('machine_hours') or 0.0)
        held['turns'] += int(row.get('requests') or 0)
        held['read'] += counts['cache_read']
        # The prompt, however it was billed. Output is what the model wrote and is not context.
        held['context'] += total - counts['output']
        held['peak'] = max(held['peak'], int(row.get('peak_context_tokens') or 0))
        rate = rates.get(row['model'])
        if rate is None:
            # A model with no rate and no tokens is the harness's `<synthetic>` label, which nobody
            # billed for and which cannot make a week unpriceable. A model with tokens and no rate
            # can, and does.
            if total:
                held['priced'] = False
            continue
        for kind, count in counts.items():
            held['usd'] += count * rate[kind] / 1_000_000.0
    out = {}
    for week, held in weeks.items():
        blended = ''
        if held['priced'] and held['tokens']:
            blended = '%.3f' % (held['usd'] * 1_000_000.0 / held['tokens'])
        out[week] = {
            'tokens': held['tokens'],
            'hours': held['hours'],
            'blended': blended,
            'turns': held['turns'],
            # Per cent to one place, not a fraction, because that is how the page quotes it and a
            # column a reader has to multiply by a hundred is a column they will misread once.
            'read_share': (100.0 * held['read'] / held['tokens']) if held['tokens'] else None,
            'context_mean': (held['context'] / held['turns']) if held['turns'] else None,
            'peak': held['peak'] or None,
        }
    return out


def decisions(tree):
    """Decision counts by status, and the honest bucket for the years before there was one.

    `design/decisions/README.md` is the index milestone 114 split `DECISIONS.md` into, and it is
    the first version of that record to carry a status per decision at all. Before it, a decision
    was a `## N.` heading in one 5,320-line file and nothing said whether it still held. Those
    weeks are counted as UNRECORDED rather than as DECIDED, because reading them as decided would
    invent a status the record never carried, which is the failure `script/names` refuses one
    directory over.
    """
    counts = dict.fromkeys(DECISION_STATUSES, 0)
    text = tree.text.get('design/decisions/README.md')
    if text:
        seen = set()
        for line in text.split('\n'):
            match = _INDEX_ROW.match(line)
            if not match:
                continue
            number, status = match.group(1), match.group(2)
            key = 'SUPERSEDED' if status.startswith('SUPERSEDED') else status
            if key in counts and number not in seen:
                seen.add(number)
                counts[key] += 1
        if sum(counts.values()):
            return counts
    legacy = tree.text.get('DECISIONS.md')
    if legacy:
        counts['UNRECORDED'] = len(set(re.findall(r'^## (\d+)\.', legacy, re.M)))
    return counts


# The four kinds of named thing `script/names` knows about, enumerated out of a tree's paths
# rather than off the disk. This is the half milestone 236's rule says stays at the caller: the
# parse of a `Name:` block is shared (`helpers/name_provenance.py`), and the file sourcing is not,
# because `script/names` is asked about the tree in front of it and this is asked about eight
# revisions nobody has checked out.
#
# A package is every `Cargo.toml` outside `crates/` and outside the workspace root, discovered
# rather than listed, which is the property `script/names` built the kind for: a package that
# arrives tomorrow is counted without editing anything. Its name is the package's, not the
# directory's, because that is the name a `use` statement and a dependency list carry.
_PACKAGE_NAME = re.compile(r'^\s*name\s*=\s*"([^"]+)"', re.M)


def name_surfaces(tree):
    """(name, path of the file that must carry its block, comment prefix), one per named thing."""
    crates, out = set(), []
    for path in tree.text:
        parts = path.split('/')
        if any(part.startswith('.') or part == 'target' for part in parts[:-1]):
            continue
        if parts[0] == 'crates' and len(parts) > 2:
            crates.add(parts[1])
        # Programs lived in `user/src/` until milestone 175 split it into `components/src/` and
        # `fixtures/src/` on 2026-09-13. This enumeration was not told, so from 2026W37 about ninety
        # programs dropped out of `names_total` (204 to 133) and the chart showed a collapse that
        # never happened, while `script/names`, which was told, went on reading 222. All three
        # directories are read because history holds both layouts and a week is counted from the
        # tree it had. The enumeration is still a second copy of `script/names`' SURFACES table;
        # the BUGS entry in this file's header says so rather than pretend it cannot drift again.
        elif (len(parts) == 3 and parts[1] == 'src' and path.endswith('.rs')
              and parts[0] in ('user', 'components', 'fixtures')):
            out.append((parts[2][:-3], path, '//!'))
        elif len(parts) == 2 and parts[0] == 'script':
            out.append((parts[1], path, '#'))
        elif parts[-1] == 'Cargo.toml' and parts[0] != 'crates':
            hit = _PACKAGE_NAME.search(tree.text[path])
            if hit:
                out.append((hit.group(1), path, '#'))
    for crate in crates:
        out.append((crate, 'crates/%s/src/lib.rs' % crate, '//!'))
    return out


def names(tree):
    """Naming provenance by status, and the count of named things it is out of.

    **`names_total` is every named thing, not the sum of the four columns**, which is the one place
    this series deviates from `milestones()` and `decisions()` above, and the deviation is the
    honest part. `ratified`, `recorded`, `provisional` and `unrecorded` are what a `Name:` block
    SAYS. A crate with no block at all has said nothing, and counting that silence as `unrecorded`
    would put a claim in the record that nobody made, which is the failure `script/names` refuses
    one directory over and the same reason `decisions()` will not read a statusless decision as
    DECIDED.

    So before milestone 115 invented the convention on 2026-08-04, the four columns are zero and
    `names_total` is the whole vocabulary of the tree, and the gap between them is the size of what
    was not yet being recorded. `script/lint` runs `script/names --check`, so in any tree from
    2026W32 onward the gap is zero and the sum is the total.

    A block that fails `script/names`' own form checks (a `ratified` with no date, a `recorded` with
    no citation) counts toward the total and toward no status, for the same reason: it has not said
    which of the four it is.
    """
    counts = dict.fromkeys(NAME_STATUSES, 0)
    surfaces = name_surfaces(tree)
    for _name, path, prefix in surfaces:
        text = tree.text.get(path)
        if text is None:
            continue
        found = name_provenance.block(text, prefix)
        if found is None:
            continue
        status, _date, _refused, problem = name_provenance.classify(found)
        if problem is None:
            counts[status] += 1
    return counts, len(surfaces)


def proposals(tree):
    """Unnumbered roadmap proposals: identified work filed where a script can see it, awaiting a number.

    **Nothing else on this dashboard can count these**, which is why the column exists.
    `milestones()` above reads milestone blocks and keys on a milestone NUMBER, and a proposal is defined by not having one, so it is invisible to that count by
    construction rather than by oversight.

    Counted the way `script/roadmap` counts them, out of the shared parse, so the two cannot
    disagree about what is in the pile. A file that does not parse as a proposal is not counted;
    `script/lint` runs `script/roadmap --check`, so in any tree since milestone 247 there are none.

    A promoted proposal is deleted rather than marked (calef, 2026-09-18), so it leaves this count
    by leaving the directory and no exclusion is needed here.

    The weeks before 2026-09-04 are zero because the directory did not exist, which is the same
    restatement artifact as the naming columns above and is rendered rather than backfilled.
    """
    count = 0
    for path, text in tree.markdown():
        directory, _slash, filename = path.rpartition('/')
        if directory != roadmap_proposals.DIRECTORY:
            continue
        if roadmap_proposals.filename_problem(filename):
            continue
        _when, _title, problem = roadmap_proposals.classify(text)
        if problem is None:
            count += 1
    return {'proposals_unnumbered': count}


def lines(tree):
    """Code and comment lines, `kernel/src` split from everything else.

    The split is the point rather than a convenience. AGENTS.md's second principle records that
    `kernel/src` measures about 40% comments deliberately, and a single tree-wide ratio hides
    exactly the thing that makes the number interesting.
    """
    out = {'kernel_code_lines': 0, 'kernel_comment_lines': 0,
           'other_code_lines': 0, 'other_comment_lines': 0}
    for path, text in tree.rust():
        total = non_blank(text)
        code = non_blank(strip_non_code(text))
        where = 'kernel' if path.startswith('kernel/src/') else 'other'
        out[where + '_code_lines'] += code
        out[where + '_comment_lines'] += total - code
    return out


# A `BUGS` section is a markdown heading in a note, a decision or a milestone block, or the same
# heading inside a Rust doc comment beside the feature it limits. Rising is GOOD: AGENTS.md calls
# these "not modesty, they are the mechanism", and a tree that grows features without growing
# honest limitations beside them is hiding them, not avoiding them.
_MD_BUGS = re.compile(r'^#{1,6} BUGS\b', re.M)
_RS_BUGS = re.compile(r'^[ \t]*(?:///|//!)[ \t]*#{1,6} BUGS\b', re.M)


def bugs(tree):
    return {'bugs_markdown': sum(len(_MD_BUGS.findall(t)) for _p, t in tree.markdown()),
            'bugs_rust': sum(len(_RS_BUGS.findall(t)) for _p, t in tree.rust())}


# The unsafe census (milestone 134 (the register of measures)) and the harness count, both from `helpers/rust_source.py`.
# The columns keep their `unsafe_` prefix here because a CSV header has no other namespace; the
# shared function's keys are the unprefixed ones a caller in `script/lint` reads.


def unsafe_census(tree):
    counts = rust_source.unsafe_census(tree.rust())
    return {'unsafe_' + key: value for key, value in counts.items()}


def unsafe_trust_census(tree):
    """The same census, split by trust boundary rather than by kernel/src/arch/.

    See `helpers/rust_source.py`'s own comment on `trust_boundary_census` for the classification
    and its caveats (`shared`, `boot_chain` and `unclassified` are populations this number cannot
    collapse into either side without guessing). Only the columns this page and a future ceiling
    could plausibly want are kept; `trust_boundary_census` also computes `*_code_lines` for
    `shared`/`boot_chain`/`unclassified`, dropped here because nothing charts them.
    """
    counts = rust_source.trust_boundary_census(tree.rust())
    return {
        'unsafe_trust_kernel': counts['kernel'],
        'unsafe_trust_kernel_code_lines': counts['kernel_code_lines'],
        'unsafe_trust_kernel_density': counts['kernel_density'],
        'unsafe_trust_userspace': counts['userspace'],
        'unsafe_trust_userspace_code_lines': counts['userspace_code_lines'],
        'unsafe_trust_userspace_density': counts['userspace_density'],
        'unsafe_trust_shared': counts['shared'],
        'unsafe_trust_boot_chain': counts['boot_chain'],
        'unsafe_trust_unclassified': counts['unclassified'],
    }


def harnesses(tree):
    counts = rust_source.harness_count(tree.rust())
    return {'harnesses_' + key: value for key, value in counts.items()}


# The prose budget: 3,000 words of main body per document, with appendices under the same cap.
# calef ratified it on 2026-09-23 (UTC) and asked for a graph in the same breath, because a ratchet
# nobody plots is a rule that holds for months with nobody able to say whether the tree is gaining
# ground or losing it. The decision is §212 (a prose budget).
#
# Two series rather than one, and they answer different questions. The excess above the cap is the
# DEBT: how many words would have to move into appendices for the tree to meet its own rule. The
# count of documents over the cap is how many places that work sits in. A tree can cut the debt by
# splitting one book and move the count not at all, and the pair is what shows that.
#
# Every document is counted, one carrying a marked exception included. The exception mechanism
# belongs to the gate, milestone 586 (a prose ratchet in lint), and a graph that subtracted exempted
# documents would hide the debt rather than measure it. If the gate wants a column for what it
# excuses, that is its own column; it does not get to edit this one.
#
# The scope is `helpers/prose_ratchet.py`'s, shared with the gate so the two cannot drift
# (milestone 586 (a prose ratchet in lint) moved it there): every `.md` directly under
# `design/`, `design/decisions/`, `design/roadmap/`, `notes/` and `briefs/`, plus `AGENTS.md`, plus
# the appendices §212 (a prose budget) puts under the same cap. Before 586 this graph's scope was "directly under"
# alone and could not see one appendix, although the docstring below said it counted them; the
# weeks since the first appendix landed (2026W39) restate to include them. `AGENTS.md` is in because
# a cap the tree's own rulebook is exempt from is not a cap; the ratified headline figures were
# measured without it, and `design/roadmap/581-one-metrics-file-per-measure.md` reconciles the two.
PROSE_CAP = prose_ratchet.PROSE_CAP


def _prose_documents(tree):
    wanted = set(prose_ratchet.documents(set(tree.text)))
    for path, text in tree.markdown():
        if path in wanted:
            yield path, text


def prose_budget(tree):
    """Words over the 3,000-word cap, and how many documents hold them.

    Whitespace-separated words over the whole file, which is what was measured and what was
    ratified. The gate (`helpers/prose_ratchet.py`) counts the same documents but strips fenced code
    and HTML comments first, so a document near the cap can read a little differently in the two;
    this keeps the ratified definition so the series is not restated a second time. An appendix is
    its own document under the same cap, which is what the ruling intends.

    The one thing both skip is a generated table (`prose_ratchet.GENERATED_TABLES`, the decisions
    index today), since nobody can cut words a script writes. That steps the series down from
    2026W39, by the index's words.

    Read from each week's own revision, unlike the cost columns: a week's prose debt is a property
    of that week's tree and git can answer for it, so `--backfill` restates the whole history.
    """
    excess = 0
    over = 0
    for _path, text in _prose_documents(tree):
        words = len(prose_ratchet.without_generated_tables(text).split())
        if words > PROSE_CAP:
            over += 1
            excess += words - PROSE_CAP
    return {'prose_excess_words': excess, 'prose_documents_over_cap': over}


# The bold backlog: §213 (writing standards) rule 3's budget of 4 bold spans per 1,000 words.
# calef ruled on 2026-09-26 (UTC) that any document a change touches must meet it, and chose not
# to sweep the rest, relying on this chart to drive it down. Two series, the prose budget's pair
# again: the spans that would have to go for every document to meet the budget, and how many
# documents they sit in.
#
# THIS SERIES IS TEMPORARY. calef, 2026-09-26: once the backlog is fixed the chart is pulled from
# the metrics. `bold_retire_line` is what makes that nobody's memory: the first week both cells are
# 0, this script prints BOLD_RETIRE and writes it onto the page beside the chart. Pulling it means
# deleting the measure, both CHARTS entries, this block, and the page section.
#
# Counted with the gate's own reader, `prose_ratchet.measure` and `prose_ratchet.bold_excess`, over
# the gate's own scope, so the chart and `script/lint` cannot disagree about what a bold span is.
# Every document counts, a marked exception included, for the prose budget's reason above.
BOLD_RETIRE = 'the bold backlog is zero; retire this chart'
BOLD_BEGIN = '<!-- bold-backlog: script/metrics writes this -->'
BOLD_END = '<!-- /bold-backlog -->'


def bold_backlog(tree):
    """Bold spans over 4 per 1,000 words, summed over documents, and how many documents hold them."""
    excess = 0
    over = 0
    for _path, text in _prose_documents(tree):
        spans = prose_ratchet.bold_excess(prose_ratchet.measure(text))
        if spans:
            over += 1
            excess += spans
    return {'bold_excess_spans': excess, 'bold_documents_over': over}


def bold_retire_line(rows):
    """BOLD_RETIRE if the latest week measured both cells at 0, else None."""
    measured = [w for w in sorted(rows) if rows[w].get('bold_excess_spans', '') not in ('', None)]
    if not measured:
        return None
    last = rows[measured[-1]]
    if str(last.get('bold_excess_spans')) == '0' and str(last.get('bold_documents_over')) == '0':
        return BOLD_RETIRE
    return None


def write_bold_status(rows):
    """The generated line beside the bold chart on the page: the week's numbers, or BOLD_RETIRE."""
    path = pathlib.Path(PAGE)
    text = path.read_text()
    if text.count(BOLD_BEGIN) != 1 or text.count(BOLD_END) != 1:
        raise SystemExit('metrics: %s must carry exactly one %r and one %r'
                         % (PAGE, BOLD_BEGIN, BOLD_END))
    measured = [w for w in sorted(rows) if rows[w].get('bold_excess_spans', '') not in ('', None)]
    if not measured:
        return
    week = measured[-1]
    line = bold_retire_line(rows)
    if line:
        body = '%s: %s.' % (week, line)
    else:
        body = ('%s: %s spans over, in %s documents.'
                % (week, rows[week]['bold_excess_spans'], rows[week]['bold_documents_over']))
    head, rest = text.split(BOLD_BEGIN)
    _old, tail = rest.split(BOLD_END)
    new = head + BOLD_BEGIN + '\n' + body + '\n' + BOLD_END + tail
    if new != text:
        path.write_text(new)


# Cumulative icount drift since a fixed anchor: milestone 415 item 3, which calef ruled on
# 2026-09-26 (UTC) is a report and not a gate (§190). The arithmetic and its selftest are
# helpers/baseline_drift.py. Three outputs, the bold backlog's shape: two cells per architecture in
# `baseline-drift.csv` (the largest upward drift, and rows past half the tripwire), one generated
# line on the page between DRIFT_BEGIN and DRIFT_END, and the whole report, every row and every
# save's `# why:` lines, regenerated as the appendix DRIFT_REPORT. The line and the appendix read
# HEAD, like the table of contents; the CSV restates each week from its own revision.
DRIFT_BEGIN = '<!-- baseline-drift: script/metrics writes this -->'
DRIFT_END = '<!-- /baseline-drift -->'
DRIFT_REPORT = DATA_DIR / 'baseline-drift.md'


# Milestone 610's generated line, the headline calef asked for, between these two markers. Four
# weeks because one week is noise at this rate (2026W37 read 6 and 2026W38 read 47) and a month is
# what a consumer of the interface would plan around.
INTERFACE_BEGIN = '<!-- interface-stability: script/metrics writes this -->'
INTERFACE_END = '<!-- /interface-stability -->'


def interface_line(rows):
    measured = [w for w in sorted(rows) if rows[w].get('interface_breaking', '') not in ('', None)]
    if not measured:
        return None
    recent = measured[-4:]

    def total(field):
        return sum(int(rows[w].get(field) or 0) for w in recent)

    commits = total('interface_contract_commits')
    crossing = total('interface_contract_commits_crossing')
    share = '; %d%% co-change' % round(100.0 * crossing / commits) if commits else ''
    unmeasured = max(int(rows[w].get('interface_crates_unmeasured') or 0) for w in recent)
    gap = '; %d crates unread' % unmeasured if unmeasured else ''
    # Short on purpose: the page is a deck with a 3,000-word budget and 7 words of headroom when
    # this panel arrived. Every column is in the CSV and the appendix.
    return ('interface, %s to %s: %d breaking changes (%d additions); %d syscall numbers changed, '
            '%d format bumps%s%s.'
            % (recent[0], recent[-1], total('interface_breaking'), total('interface_additions'),
               total('interface_syscall_changed'), total('interface_format_bumps'), share, gap))


def write_interface_line(rows):
    path = pathlib.Path(PAGE)
    text = path.read_text()
    if text.count(INTERFACE_BEGIN) != 1 or text.count(INTERFACE_END) != 1:
        raise SystemExit('metrics: %s must carry exactly one %r and one %r'
                         % (PAGE, INTERFACE_BEGIN, INTERFACE_END))
    line = interface_line(rows)
    if line is None:
        return
    before, rest = text.split(INTERFACE_BEGIN)
    _old, after = rest.split(INTERFACE_END)
    new = before + INTERFACE_BEGIN + '\n' + line + '\n' + INTERFACE_END + after
    if new != text:
        path.write_text(new)


def write_drift_report():
    """The page's generated drift line and the appendix beside it, both from HEAD."""
    reports, stamp = baseline_drift.report('HEAD')
    head = git('rev-parse', 'HEAD').decode().strip()
    week = week_of(git('show', '-s', '--format=%cI', head).decode().strip())
    path = pathlib.Path(PAGE)
    text = path.read_text()
    if text.count(DRIFT_BEGIN) != 1 or text.count(DRIFT_END) != 1:
        raise SystemExit('metrics: %s must carry exactly one %r and one %r'
                         % (PAGE, DRIFT_BEGIN, DRIFT_END))
    before, rest = text.split(DRIFT_BEGIN)
    _old, after = rest.split(DRIFT_END)
    new = (before + DRIFT_BEGIN + '\n' + baseline_drift.status(reports, week) + '\n'
           + DRIFT_END + after)
    if new != text:
        path.write_text(new)
    body = baseline_drift.render(reports, stamp)
    if not DRIFT_REPORT.exists() or DRIFT_REPORT.read_text() != body:
        DRIFT_REPORT.write_text(body)


# The ratified field and its three values, as `script/fatal-risks` gates them. calef ruled on
# 2026-09-23 and `design/fatal-risks.md`'s vocabulary section carries the argument; the enumeration
# is duplicated here rather than imported because `script/fatal-risks` is a shell wrapper around a
# heredoc with nothing importable in it, and because this reader is asked about HISTORY, where the
# words were different. A word that leaves the ratified set breaks the gate before it reaches here.
FATAL_RISK_STATUS = re.compile(r'^\*\*Experiment status: ([A-Z][A-Z-]*)\b', re.M)

# **What the five retired words meant, for reading past revisions only.** This is a translation
# table over history, not a second vocabulary: nothing may be written in these words today, and
# `script/fatal-risks` fails on any of them. They are here so the series says something true about
# the weeks before the ruling instead of reporting a tree that had no field as a tree with nine
# unanswered risks. `MEASURED` and `AUDITED` were always claims about how WELL an experiment was
# done, and an experiment done well is one that ran.
RETIRED_FATAL_RISK_STATUS = re.compile(r'^\*\*Status: ([A-Z]+)', re.M)
RETIRED_MEANING = {'RUN': 'run', 'MEASURED': 'run', 'AUDITED': 'run',
                   'UNRUN': 'not_run', 'NOT': 'not_run', 'UNTESTED': 'cannot_run'}


def fatal_risks(tree):
    """The nine claims that would kill nife, by ratified Experiment status.

    **This replaces a tested/untested pair, and the refusal it replaces was right when it was
    written.** That docstring said a verdict column "would be reading a sentence and guessing",
    because three of the four statuses then written said GREEN or AMBER in capitals and risk 7's
    said neither. Nothing about that reading has been overturned. What changed on 2026-09-23 is the
    thing being read: calef ratified a closed field, `Experiment status`, with exactly three values,
    and `script/fatal-risks` now fails on a fourth. So this parses an enumeration rather than a
    sentence, which is a different act from the one that was refused.

    **It is still not a verdict series, and that is deliberate.** What an experiment FOUND stays in
    the file's prose, where `GREEN`, `AMBER`, `MEASURED` and `AUDITED` already are. A chart of
    findings was the other half of the proposal raised on 2026-09-23 and calef did not take it.

    **Why the pair had to go.** All nine entries carried a status line by 2026-09-23, so
    `fatal_risks_tested` sat at nine and `fatal_risks_untested` at zero and the chart was a flat
    line carrying no information. The new series can move in both directions and has a third value
    the old pair could not express, which is the one risk 8 needs: an experiment nobody may run is
    not a kind of result.

    **Weeks before the field existed are read through `RETIRED_MEANING`**, and a risk with no status
    line at all counts in none of the three. That is the honest shape: an entry that said nothing
    said nothing.

    **`fatal_risks_total` is every risk in that week's file, not the sum of the three**, which is
    `names()`' shape for the same problem. Without it the chart showed five, four and five risks in
    2026W36 to 2026W38 and a reader took it for the count of risks, when all nine had been on `main`
    since pull request #588 landed on 2026-08-31 UTC. Risks 4, 5, 8 and 9 had no status line then,
    and risk 3 said `STALE` in 2026W37, a word `RETIRED_MEANING` does not translate because it said
    the old result had expired rather than which of the three the risk was. The gap between the total
    and the three columns is drawn as `unclassified` and not stored, as `names_no_block` is, because
    it is not a value `Experiment status` can hold.
    """
    counts = {'fatal_risks_run': 0, 'fatal_risks_not_run': 0, 'fatal_risks_cannot_run': 0,
              'fatal_risks_total': 0}
    text = tree.text.get('design/fatal-risks.md')
    if not text:
        return counts
    parts = re.split(r'^## (\d+)\.', text, flags=re.M)
    for i in range(1, len(parts), 2):
        body = parts[i + 1]
        counts['fatal_risks_total'] += 1
        m = FATAL_RISK_STATUS.search(body)
        if m:
            key = m.group(1).lower().replace('-', '_')
        else:
            old = RETIRED_FATAL_RISK_STATUS.search(body)
            key = RETIRED_MEANING.get(old.group(1)) if old else None
        if key and 'fatal_risks_' + key in counts:
            counts['fatal_risks_' + key] += 1
    return counts


def coverage_from_lcov(path):
    """Aggregate line coverage from an lcov file, as a percentage to one decimal.

    LF and LH are lines-found and lines-hit per file, which is the same pair `script/coverage`'s
    per-file floor reads. This is the aggregate rather than the floor: the floor's job is to make
    one bad file loud, and a trend wants the whole.

    **The minimum is deliberately not computed here**, though the numbers to do it are in this same
    file. It would be a different minimum: `script/coverage` exempts files that cannot execute a
    line on the host, and the lowest file in an unfiltered lcov is one of those, so the answer would
    be a number no build can ever fail on. `coverage_min_from_summary` below reads the gate's own.
    """
    found = hit = 0
    for line in pathlib.Path(path).read_text(errors='replace').split('\n'):
        if line.startswith('LF:'):
            found += int(line[3:])
        elif line.startswith('LH:'):
            hit += int(line[3:])
    if not found:
        raise SystemExit('metrics: %s records no lines; is it an lcov file?' % path)
    return '%.1f' % (100.0 * hit / found)


def coverage_min_from_summary(path):
    """The minimum per-file line coverage, read from what `script/coverage` wrote.

    Plain `key value` lines. Only `min_file_pct` is plotted; the rest of the file (which file, how
    many were gated, how many a floor of 85 or 90 would newly fail) is there for a person deciding
    whether to raise the floor, and is not a series.
    """
    for line in pathlib.Path(path).read_text(errors='replace').split('\n'):
        if line.startswith('min_file_pct '):
            return '%.1f' % float(line.split()[1])
    raise SystemExit('metrics: %s records no min_file_pct; is it script/coverage floor summary?'
                     % path)


def row_for(rev, week, coverage='', person_weeks='', coverage_min=''):
    tree = Tree(rev)
    when = git('show', '-s', '--format=%cI', rev).decode().strip()
    row = {'week': week, 'commit': rev[:12], 'date': when[:10],
           'coverage_lines_pct': coverage,
           'coverage_min_file_pct': coverage_min,
           'human_person_weeks': person_weeks or DEFAULT_PERSON_WEEKS}
    ms = milestones(tree)
    for status in MILESTONE_STATUSES:
        row[_col('milestones', status)] = ms[status]
    row['milestones_total'] = sum(ms.values())
    dec = decisions(tree)
    for status in DECISION_STATUSES:
        row[_col('decisions', status)] = dec[status]
    row['decisions_total'] = sum(dec.values())
    nm, named = names(tree)
    for status in NAME_STATUSES:
        row[_col('names', status)] = nm[status]
    row['names_total'] = named
    for part in (proposals(tree), lines(tree), bugs(tree), harnesses(tree),
                 unsafe_census(tree), unsafe_trust_census(tree), fatal_risks(tree),
                 prose_budget(tree), bold_backlog(tree)):
        row.update(part)
    row.update(baseline_drift.series_cells(rev))
    return row


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Weeks, and which commit stands for one.

def week_of(iso_datetime):
    when = datetime.fromisoformat(iso_datetime).astimezone(timezone.utc)
    year, week, _day = when.isocalendar()
    return '%04dW%02d' % (year, week)


def week_of_date(stamp):
    """The ISO week of a bare `YYYY-MM-DD`, with no timezone arithmetic.

    `week_of` above takes a git timestamp and converts it to UTC, which is right for a commit. A
    date written into the roadmap is already UTC (`AGENTS.md`, every date in this tree is UTC), and
    running it through a local-time conversion would move a Monday's milestone into the previous
    week on this machine and not on CI.
    """
    year, week, _day = date.fromisoformat(stamp).isocalendar()
    return '%04dW%02d' % (year, week)


def week_commits():
    """{ISO week: the first-parent commit that stands for it}, oldest first.

    The greatest committer date inside the week, on the first-parent trunk. First-parent because
    that is the tree a reader means by "on that date"; committer date because a merge's author date
    can be days old, which would file a merge under the week its branch was cut.
    """
    out = {}
    log = git('log', '--first-parent', '--format=%H %cI', 'HEAD').decode().split('\n')
    for entry in log:
        if not entry.strip():
            continue
        sha, when = entry.split()
        out.setdefault(week_of(when), sha)   # log is newest-first, so the first seen is the latest
    return dict(sorted(out.items()))


def read_csv():
    """Every measure file merged into one row per week: `{week: {column: cell}}`.

    The join is on `WEEK` alone, which is why every file spells and formats it identically. A week
    a measure has no row for contributes no keys, so the cell comes back absent rather than empty,
    and `_cell` and `row.get` already treat those the same. That is what lets a series start at the
    week it was first measured instead of carrying blanks back to 2026W29.

    A column is read only from the file `MEASURES` assigns it to. A stray column left in another
    file by a hand edit or a half-finished rename is ignored rather than silently winning, and the
    next `--update` drops it.
    """
    rows = {}
    for name, columns in MEASURES:
        path = measure_path(name)
        if not path.exists():
            continue
        text = path.read_text().strip().split('\n')
        header = text[0].split(',')
        for line in text[1:]:
            if not line.strip():
                continue
            cells = dict(zip(header, line.split(',')))
            week = cells.get(WEEK)
            if not week:
                continue
            row = rows.setdefault(week, {WEEK: week})
            for column in columns:
                if column in cells:
                    row[column] = cells[column]
    return rows


def render_csvs(rows):
    """`{path: text}` for every measure, sorted, fixed columns, fixed formatting.

    Never appended to. This is the whole of the idempotence guarantee: the week being written
    replaces its own entry rather than adding one, so running twice in a week produces the same
    bytes, and so does running the backfill against a history that has not moved.

    A week with nothing to say is omitted rather than written blank, and that is the point of the
    split rather than a tidiness. A measure introduced in 2026W39 used to leave ten empty cells
    behind it in a wide file, and an empty cell is ambiguous between "measured zero" and "nobody
    looked"; the charts needed an `absent` flag per column to tell a reader which. An absent row
    says only the true thing. A week the measure answered for partially still gets its row, with
    the cell it could not fill left empty, because there the ambiguity is real and local.
    """
    DATA_DIR.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    out = {}
    for name, columns in MEASURES:
        lines_out = [','.join([WEEK] + columns)]
        for week in sorted(rows):
            row = rows[week]
            cells = [str(row.get(column, '')) for column in columns]
            if not any(cells):
                continue
            lines_out.append(','.join([week] + cells))
        out[measure_path(name)] = '\n'.join(lines_out) + '\n'
    return out


def write_csvs(rendered):
    for path, text in rendered.items():
        path.write_text(text)


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The charts.
#
# GitHub will not render an interactive chart, and it will not run a script in a markdown page, so
# a committed SVG is the only thing a reader actually sees. Written by hand rather than by a
# plotting crate: DECISIONS §46 (thin primitives or whole subsystems, and we write everything in
# between) makes taking one a decision, and a stacked bar is arithmetic and
# string formatting. The side benefit is that a diff can review one, which a binary PNG refuses.
#
# Colours are one categorical order with a LIGHT and a DARK step per hue, chosen for each surface
# rather than one set of hexes asked to serve both. Validated on 2026-09-19 with the dataviz
# skill's six-check validator against GitHub's own surfaces (#ffffff, #0d1117): every check passes
# in both modes, worst adjacent colour-blind separation 9.1 light and 8.4 dark (OKLab x100, target
# 8). This replaced Okabe-Ito, whose reputation is colour-blind separation and not contrast: its
# yellow read at 1.32:1 on white, which made the milestones chart's "Removed" band nearly
# invisible, and its gray failed the chroma floor. Three light steps still sit under 3:1 on white,
# which is legal only with relief, and each chart carries three kinds: the legend, a <title> on
# every segment, and the measure files as the table view. Slots are in this fixed order and never
# cycled. The eighth slot was added on 2026-09-19 for the milestones chart's proposals, appended
# rather than inserted so that no existing series changed colour, and all eight were validated
# again in both modes before it shipped. A ninth series is never a new hue; it folds into another.

PALETTE_LIGHT = ['#2a78d6', '#eb6834', '#1baf7a', '#eda100', '#e87ba4', '#008300', '#4a3aa7',
                 '#e34948']
PALETTE_DARK = ['#3987e5', '#d95926', '#199e70', '#c98500', '#d55181', '#008300', '#9085e9',
                '#e66767']

CHART_W, CHART_H = 860, 380
# The charts show the most recent CHART_WEEKS weeks; the measure files keep every week, and a
# still restates all of them. calef, 2026-09-19: ten weeks, so W29 leaves the charts when W39
# arrives. Old weeks are not deleted, only no longer drawn, because the CSV is the table view
# the charts' relief rule leans on and a table that forgets is not one.
CHART_WEEKS = 10
PAD_L, PAD_R, PAD_T, PAD_B = 64, 210, 58, 56
LEGEND_WRAP = 22   # characters per legend line before a label wraps to a second


def esc(text):
    return (text.replace('&', '&amp;').replace('<', '&lt;').replace('>', '&gt;'))


def _wrap(text, width):
    """Split a legend label on word boundaries into lines of at most `width` characters.

    The legend column clipped six labels on 2026-09-19 because the width a label needs depends on
    whichever font the viewer's machine substitutes, and the arithmetic had assumed a narrow one.
    Wrapping is robust to that; a guess at glyph widths is not.
    """
    lines, line = [], ''
    for word in text.split():
        if line and len(line) + 1 + len(word) > width:
            lines.append(line)
            line = word
        else:
            line = word if not line else line + ' ' + word
    return lines + ([line] if line else [])


def week_label(week):
    """The key is the label. `2026W36` on the axis, in the CSV, and in the prose.

    calef ratified one spelling on 2026-09-02, after the page carried three: `2026-W30` in the
    narrative, `202636` on an axis, `2026-W29` in the data. This function exists so that if a
    future spelling is wanted there is exactly one place to change it, and so the axis cannot
    drift from the key it is derived from.
    """
    return week


def svg_chart(title, subtitle, weeks, series, path, ceiling=None, absent=None, trend=None):
    """One stacked bar per week, one colour per series, a legend on the right.

    `series` is [(label, [value per week])]. A single-element series is an ordinary bar chart,
    which is what the density and coverage panels are. `ceiling` is a number, labelled
    `ceiling`, or a (number, word) pair when the dashed line is something else: coverage's is
    `script/coverage`'s per-file floor, and labelling a floor a ceiling says the opposite.

    `absent` is a per-week flag for **a week that was not measured, as against a week that measured
    zero**. Both draw no bar, and a reader cannot tell them apart, which for milestone 519's cost
    columns is the difference between an honest gap and a lie that gets averaged into a published
    figure. A flagged week gets a muted marker in the plot and a `<title>` saying so. Coverage's
    2026W29 is the same shape and settled for a sentence in its subtitle; this makes it visible on
    the chart, where the reader is.

    `trend` is a line over the bars, (label, short label, [(value, full) per week]), from
    `trailing_mean`. It is drawn in the page's text colour rather than a palette slot, because the
    palette is for series and this is arithmetic over one of them; a full window is a solid line and
    a short one is dashed, each with its own legend entry, so the two are never read as the same.
    """
    line_word = 'ceiling'
    if isinstance(ceiling, tuple):
        ceiling, line_word = ceiling
    totals = [sum(s[1][i] for s in series) for i in range(len(weeks))]
    top = max(totals + ([ceiling] if ceiling else []) + [1]
              + ([v for v, _full in trend[2]] if trend else []))
    # An axis a person reads: gridlines at 1, 2, 2.5 or 5 times a power of ten, and whichever of
    # four, five or six intervals wastes the least height. Four alone rounds 231 up to 400.
    axis_top, ticks = None, 4
    for count in (4, 5, 6):
        raw = top / count
        magnitude = 10.0 ** math.floor(math.log10(raw)) if raw > 0 else 1.0
        for factor in (1, 2, 2.5, 5, 10):
            if magnitude * factor >= raw:
                candidate = magnitude * factor * count
                break
        if axis_top is None or candidate < axis_top:
            axis_top, ticks = candidate, count
    plot_w = CHART_W - PAD_L - PAD_R
    plot_h = CHART_H - PAD_T - PAD_B
    slot = plot_w / max(len(weeks), 1)
    bar_w = min(slot * 0.66, 54)

    def y_of(value):
        return PAD_T + plot_h - plot_h * value / axis_top

    out = ['<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="%d" height="%d" '
           'viewBox="0 0 %d %d" role="img" aria-label="%s">'
           % (CHART_W, CHART_H, CHART_W, CHART_H, esc(title)),
           '<style>',
           '  .bg{fill:#ffffff} .fg{fill:#1f2328} .mut{fill:#59636e} .grid{stroke:#d1d9e0}',
           '  .seg{stroke:#ffffff;stroke-width:1}'
           + (' .tr{stroke:#1f2328;fill:none} .trd{fill:#1f2328}' if trend else ''),
           # A halo in the surface colour behind each value label, so a total that lands on the
           # dashed ceiling (the unsafe chart's 77s against its 88) stays legible.
           '  .val{paint-order:stroke;stroke:#ffffff;stroke-width:3px;stroke-linejoin:round}',
           ''.join('  .s%d{fill:%s}' % (j, c) for j, c in enumerate(PALETTE_LIGHT)),
           '  @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark){',
           '    .bg{fill:#0d1117} .fg{fill:#e6edf3} .mut{fill:#9198a1} .grid{stroke:#30363d}',
           '    .seg{stroke:#0d1117} .val{stroke:#0d1117}'
           + (' .tr{stroke:#e6edf3} .trd{fill:#e6edf3}' if trend else '')
           + ''.join(' .s%d{fill:%s}' % (j, c) for j, c in enumerate(PALETTE_DARK)) + '}',
           '  text{font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,"Segoe UI",Helvetica,Arial,'
           'sans-serif}',
           '</style>',
           '<rect class="bg" width="%d" height="%d"/>' % (CHART_W, CHART_H),
           '<text class="fg" x="%d" y="26" font-size="16" font-weight="600">%s</text>'
           % (PAD_L - 44, esc(title)),
           '<text class="mut" x="%d" y="44" font-size="11">%s</text>'
           % (PAD_L - 44, esc(subtitle))]

    for tick in range(ticks + 1):
        value = axis_top * tick / ticks
        y = y_of(value)
        out.append('<line class="grid" x1="%d" y1="%.1f" x2="%.1f" y2="%.1f" '
                   'stroke-width="1"/>' % (PAD_L, y, PAD_L + plot_w, y))
        label = '%g' % value if value == int(value) else '%.1f' % value
        out.append('<text class="mut" x="%d" y="%.1f" font-size="10" text-anchor="end">%s</text>'
                   % (PAD_L - 8, y + 3, label))

    # The ceiling is drawn BEFORE the bars and their value labels, so a total landing on it
    # sits on top of the line rather than under it. It used to be drawn last and struck
    # through the unsafe chart's 77s, three of them, sitting just under its 88.
    if ceiling:
        y = y_of(ceiling)
        out.append('<line x1="%d" y1="%.1f" x2="%.1f" y2="%.1f" stroke="#D55E00" '
                   'stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="5 4"/>'
                   % (PAD_L, y, PAD_L + plot_w, y))
        out.append('<text class="mut" x="%.1f" y="%.1f" font-size="10">%s %s</text>'
                   % (PAD_L + plot_w + 6, y + 3, line_word, _number(ceiling)))

    for i, week in enumerate(weeks):
        x = PAD_L + slot * i + (slot - bar_w) / 2
        base = 0.0
        top_j = max((j for j, (_l, v) in enumerate(series) if v[i]), default=None)
        for j, (label, values) in enumerate(series):
            value = values[i]
            if not value:
                continue
            height = plot_h * value / axis_top
            y = y_of(base + value)
            # Every segment is stroked in the surface colour, which leaves a 2px gap between
            # neighbours so a stack reads as parts even where two fills are close in lightness.
            # Only the top of the whole bar is rounded: rounding every segment would make a
            # stack look like beads.
            tip = '<title>%s, %s: %s</title>' % (esc(week_label(week)), esc(label), _number(value))
            r = min(4.0, height / 2, bar_w / 2) if j == top_j else 0.0
            if r:
                d = ('M%.1f,%.1f V%.1f Q%.1f,%.1f %.1f,%.1f H%.1f Q%.1f,%.1f %.1f,%.1f V%.1f Z'
                     % (x, y + height, y + r, x, y, x + r, y, x + bar_w - r,
                        x + bar_w, y, x + bar_w, y + r, y + height))
                out.append('<path class="seg s%d" d="%s">%s</path>' % (j, d, tip))
            else:
                out.append('<rect class="seg s%d" x="%.1f" y="%.1f" width="%.1f" height="%.1f">'
                           '%s</rect>' % (j, x, y, bar_w, height, tip))
            base += value
        out.append('<text class="mut" x="%.1f" y="%d" font-size="10" text-anchor="middle">%s</text>'
                   % (x + bar_w / 2, PAD_T + plot_h + 18, esc(week_label(week))))
        if totals[i]:
            out.append('<text class="fg val" x="%.1f" y="%.1f" font-size="10" '
                       'text-anchor="middle">%s</text>'
                       % (x + bar_w / 2, y_of(totals[i]) - 5, _number(totals[i])))
        elif absent and absent[i]:
            # Words rather than a dash at the foot of the empty slot. This is the one place on
            # this page where a reader is entitled to read "nothing here" as "nobody measured"
            # rather than "zero", so it says which, and it says it in the chart rather than only in
            # the tooltip, because a tooltip does not exist on a phone or in a screenshot.
            out.append('<text class="mut" x="%.1f" y="%.1f" font-size="9" '
                       'text-anchor="middle">not<tspan x="%.1f" dy="10">measured</tspan>'
                       '<title>%s: not measured</title></text>'
                       % (x + bar_w / 2, PAD_T + plot_h - 16, x + bar_w / 2,
                          esc(week_label(week))))

    if trend:
        label, short, points = trend
        centre = [PAD_L + slot * i + slot / 2 for i in range(len(weeks))]
        for i in range(1, len(points)):
            dash = '' if points[i][1] else ' stroke-dasharray="4 3"'
            out.append('<line class="tr" x1="%.1f" y1="%.1f" x2="%.1f" y2="%.1f" '
                       'stroke-width="2"%s/>' % (centre[i - 1], y_of(points[i - 1][0]),
                                                 centre[i], y_of(points[i][0]), dash))
        for i, (value, full) in enumerate(points):
            out.append('<circle class="trd" cx="%.1f" cy="%.1f" r="3"><title>%s, %s: %.1f'
                       '</title></circle>' % (centre[i], y_of(value), esc(week_label(weeks[i])),
                                             esc(label if full else short), value))

    legend_x = CHART_W - PAD_R + 22
    y = PAD_T + 6
    if trend:
        kinds = [(label, True)] if any(f for _v, f in trend[2]) else []
        if not all(f for _v, f in trend[2]):
            kinds.append((trend[1], False))
        for text, full in kinds:
            out.append('<line class="tr" x1="%d" y1="%d" x2="%d" y2="%d" stroke-width="2"%s/>'
                       % (legend_x, y + 5, legend_x + 11, y + 5,
                          '' if full else ' stroke-dasharray="4 3"'))
            lines = _wrap(text, LEGEND_WRAP)
            spans = ''.join('<tspan x="%d" dy="%s">%s</tspan>'
                            % (legend_x + 17, '0' if k == 0 else '13', esc(line))
                            for k, line in enumerate(lines))
            out.append('<text class="fg" y="%d" font-size="11">%s</text>' % (y + 10, spans))
            y += 19 + 13 * (len(lines) - 1)
    for j, (label, _values) in enumerate(series):
        out.append('<rect class="s%d" x="%d" y="%d" width="11" height="11" rx="2"/>'
                   % (j, legend_x, y))
        lines = _wrap(label, LEGEND_WRAP)
        spans = ''.join('<tspan x="%d" dy="%s">%s</tspan>'
                        % (legend_x + 17, '0' if k == 0 else '13', esc(line))
                        for k, line in enumerate(lines))
        out.append('<text class="fg" y="%d" font-size="11">%s</text>' % (y + 10, spans))
        y += 19 + 13 * (len(lines) - 1)
    out.append('</svg>')
    path.write_text('\n'.join(out) + '\n')


def _number(value):
    value = float(value)
    return '%d' % value if value == int(value) else '%.1f' % value


def _cell(row, field):
    raw = row.get(field, '')
    return float(raw) if raw not in ('', None) else 0.0


# One band on this page is arithmetic over columns rather than a column of its own. The naming
# chart's "no provenance block" is `names_total` minus the four statuses, and it is deliberately not
# in the CSV: it is not a status a `Name:` block can carry, and a fifth column would put a fifth
# word in a four-word vocabulary that `script/names` gates on. Drawn rather than stored, because the
# three weeks before milestone 115 invented the convention would otherwise be three empty bars,
# which this page's own rule reads as "the record did not exist" and would leave the reader with no
# idea that the tree already had 119 named things in it.
def _tokens_per_milestone(row):
    """Millions of tokens per milestone built that week, or zero when either side is missing.

    **A ratio rather than a total, which is milestone 519's own requirement**: a total says the
    project was busy, and a ratio says whether the method is getting cheaper, which is the claim the
    project actually makes. Zero is returned for a week with no capture and for a week that built
    nothing, and the chart's `absent` flag is what tells those two apart for the reader.

    The denominator is the milestone count, with every caveat that series already carries: a
    milestone is not a fixed unit, and a week of bookkeeping moves the ratio without anything
    getting cheaper or dearer. Read the shape.
    """
    built = _cell(row, 'milestones_built_this_week')
    tokens = _cell(row, 'lane_tokens')
    if not built or not tokens:
        return 0.0
    return tokens / 1_000_000.0 / built


def _fold(kind):
    """The chart's `other models` band: every model outside the four that have a hue.

    The palette has eight fixed slots and its own comment rules that a ninth series folds into
    another rather than inventing a colour. The CSV keeps `opus_4_8`, `fable_5_1` and
    `other_models` apart, because that is where a reader checks arithmetic; the chart adds them,
    because a band of seven commits is a legend entry and not a shape.

    `opus_4_8` folded here on 2026-09-24, when Opus 5.5 needed a hue and the commits chart was
    already at eight. Opus 5.5 took Opus 4.8's slot, so no other series changed colour. Opus 4.8
    was the one to fold because it has signed nothing since 2026W38, while Sonnet 5 (fewer commits
    in total) is still signing them. Both charts fold the same set so that one hue means one model
    on either.
    """
    def derive(row):
        return sum(_cell(row, _model_col(kind, s))
                   for s in ('opus_4_8', 'fable_5_1', 'other_models'))
    return derive


DERIVED = {
    'model_commits_other_shown': _fold('commits'),
    'model_lines_other_shown': _fold('lines'),
    'fatal_risks_unclassified': lambda row: max(
        0.0, _cell(row, 'fatal_risks_total') - sum(_cell(row, 'fatal_risks_' + s)
                                                   for s in ('run', 'not_run', 'cannot_run'))),
    'names_no_block': lambda row: max(
        0.0, _cell(row, 'names_total') - sum(_cell(row, _col('names', s)) for s in NAME_STATUSES)),
    'tokens_per_milestone': _tokens_per_milestone,
}


def series_of(rows, weeks, columns, scale=1.0):
    out = []
    for label, field in columns:
        values = []
        for week in weeks:
            derive = DERIVED.get(field)
            raw = derive(rows[week]) if derive else _cell(rows[week], field)
            values.append(raw / scale)
        if any(values):
            out.append((label, values))
    return out


# Each entry is (file, title, subtitle, columns, scale, ceiling, absent_field). `absent_field` names
# a CSV column whose empty cell means "not measured" rather than "zero"; the chart then marks that
# week instead of leaving a gap a reader will read as a zero. Only the columns that can genuinely be
# absent carry one, which is milestone 519's cost pair and coverage.
CHARTS = [
    ('milestones.svg', 'Milestones by status, and proposals waiting for a number',
     'the blocks in design/roadmap/ (design/roadmap.md before the one-file-per-milestone split); '
     'unnumbered proposals from design/roadmap/proposals/ stacked on top',
     [('Built', 'milestones_built'), ('Partial', 'milestones_partial'),
      ('In progress', 'milestones_in_progress'), ('Not started', 'milestones_not_started'),
      ('Optional', 'milestones_optional'), ('Recorded', 'milestones_recorded'),
      ('Removed', 'milestones_removed'),
      ('Proposal, no number yet', 'proposals_unnumbered')], 1.0, None, None),
    ('decisions.svg', 'Architecture decisions by status',
     'design/decisions/README.md; before milestone 114 split it, a decision carried no status',
     [('Decided', 'decisions_decided'), ('Amended', 'decisions_amended'),
      ('Superseded', 'decisions_superseded'), ('Proposed', 'decisions_proposed'),
      ('No status recorded', 'decisions_unrecorded')], 1.0, None, None),
    ('names.svg', 'Names by what the tree records about them',
     'crates, programs, script/ entry points and Cargo packages; provenance lives in the header',
     [('Ratified', 'names_ratified'), ('Recorded', 'names_recorded'),
      ('Provisional', 'names_provisional'), ('Unrecorded', 'names_unrecorded'),
      ('No provenance block', 'names_no_block')], 1.0, None, None),
    ('lines.svg', 'Rust in the tree, in thousands of lines',
     'kernel/src split out, because it is commented on purpose; vendor/ excluded',
     [('kernel/src code', 'kernel_code_lines'), ('kernel/src comments', 'kernel_comment_lines'),
      ('other code', 'other_code_lines'), ('other comments', 'other_comment_lines')],
     1000.0, None, None),
    ('bugs.svg', 'BUGS sections, where a reader meets the feature',
     'rising is good: an honest limitation beside the thing it limits',
     [('in markdown', 'bugs_markdown'), ('in Rust doc comments', 'bugs_rust')], 1.0, None,
     None),
    ('harnesses.svg', 'Kani proof harnesses, and what can falsify them',
     'fatal risk 2: a proof nobody can turn red is not evidence',
     [('falsification on record', 'harnesses_falsified'),
      ('unfalsified', 'harnesses_unfalsified')], 1.0, None, None),
    ('unsafe.svg', 'unsafe blocks per 10,000 lines outside kernel/src/arch/',
     'the direction is down; the ceiling is script/lint’s',
     [('blocks per 10,000 lines', 'unsafe_density')], 1.0, 88, None),
    ('unsafe-trust.svg', 'The same unsafe blocks, by trust boundary',
     'kernel: no confinement over it. userspace: confined like any other program. see notes/'
     'project-metrics.md for shared/boot chain/unclassified and why this is not the chart above',
     [('kernel (trusted base)', 'unsafe_trust_kernel'),
      ('userspace (confined)', 'unsafe_trust_userspace'),
      ('shared (both, unresolved)', 'unsafe_trust_shared'),
      ('boot chain (uefi_loader)', 'unsafe_trust_boot_chain'),
      ('unclassified', 'unsafe_trust_unclassified')], 1.0, None, None),
    ('fatal-risks.svg', 'The nine things that would kill nife, by Experiment status',
     'design/fatal-risks.md; this says whether an experiment happened, never what it found. The '
     'findings are in that file, in prose',
     [('RUN', 'fatal_risks_run'), ('NOT-RUN', 'fatal_risks_not_run'),
      ('CANNOT-RUN', 'fatal_risks_cannot_run'),
      ('unclassified', 'fatal_risks_unclassified')],
     1.0, None, None),
    ('velocity.svg', 'Milestones built each week',
     'a flow, not a stock: what the index says turned BUILT in those seven days',
     [('built that week', 'milestones_built_this_week')], 1.0, None, None),
    ('pull-requests.svg', 'Pull requests merged each week',
     'first-parent merge commits on main; a milestone is one of these, and most of these are not',
     [('merged that week', 'merged_pull_requests')], 1.0, None, None),
    ('models-commits.svg', 'Commits each week, by the model that signed them',
     'from the Co-Authored-By trailer; attributed + unattributed + merge is every commit that '
     'week, and a merge is a pull request landing rather than a piece of writing',
     [('Opus 5', 'model_commits_opus_5'), ('Opus 5.5', 'model_commits_opus_5_5'),
      ('Sonnet 5', 'model_commits_sonnet_5'), ('Fable 5', 'model_commits_fable_5'),
      ('other models', 'model_commits_other_shown'),
      ('unattributed', 'model_commits_unattributed'),
      ('before the convention', 'model_commits_pre_convention'),
      ('merge (a pull request landing)', 'model_commits_merge')], 1.0, None,
     'model_commits_unattributed'),
    ('models-lines.svg', 'Lines touched each week, by the model that signed them, in thousands',
     'added plus removed, not net. VOLUME, NOT EFFORT: the hardest change of 2026-09-23 was a '
     'dozen lines and the largest was a mechanical sweep. Merges carry no line count',
     [('Opus 5', 'model_lines_opus_5'), ('Opus 5.5', 'model_lines_opus_5_5'),
      ('Sonnet 5', 'model_lines_sonnet_5'), ('Fable 5', 'model_lines_fable_5'),
      ('other models', 'model_lines_other_shown'),
      ('unattributed', 'model_lines_unattributed'),
      ('before the convention', 'model_lines_pre_convention')], 1000.0, None,
     'model_lines_unattributed'),
    ('effort.svg', 'Machine effort per milestone built, in millions of tokens',
     'a ratio, not a total: down means the method got cheaper. Weeks with no surviving records are '
     'marked, not zeroed',
     [('million tokens per milestone', 'tokens_per_milestone')], 1.0, None, 'lane_tokens'),
    ('context-per-turn.svg', 'What a turn carried, in thousands of tokens of context',
     'mean prompt per request, from the same records as the row above; the bill tracks this, not '
     'the length of the answer. Weeks with no surviving records are marked, not zeroed',
     [('thousand tokens of context per turn', 'lane_context_per_turn_mean')], 1000.0, None,
     'lane_context_per_turn_mean'),
    ('coverage.svg', 'Host-crate line coverage, per cent',
     'each week by its own script/coverage; 2026W29 predates the script',
     [('line coverage', 'coverage_lines_pct')], 1.0, (80, 'floor'), 'coverage_lines_pct'),
    # The aggregate above is the trend; this is the number that can fail a build. They move
    # independently, which is the whole reason for a second panel: a workspace can hold at 94.7%
    # while one file slides from 85% to 81% and the chart above does not twitch. Blank weeks are
    # honest rather than new: a per-file minimum needs an lcov for that week, and only the
    # aggregate was ever kept, so the series starts where the recording starts.
    ('coverage-floor.svg', 'Lowest-covered single file, per cent',
     'the number script/coverage gates on; 2026W31-W38 backfilled 2026-09-24, W30 predates the floor',
     [('minimum per-file coverage', 'coverage_min_file_pct')], 1.0, (80, 'floor'),
     'coverage_min_file_pct'),
    # Two panels for one measure, the coverage pair's reason: these series stack with nothing, and
    # with each other least of all, since one is words and one is a count of files. The debt is the
    # headline and the document count is where the work sits; the tree can halve one without moving
    # the other, and a reader has to see both to know which happened.
    ('prose-budget.svg', 'Words over the 3,000-word cap, in thousands',
     'the debt the 3,000-word cap names: what would move into appendices. Every document counts, an '
     'exempted one included, because the graph exists to show the debt rather than excuse it',
     [('words over the cap', 'prose_excess_words')], 1000.0, None, 'prose_excess_words'),
    ('prose-budget-documents.svg', 'Documents over the 3,000-word cap',
     'the same debt by where it sits; one split book moves the chart above and not this one',
     [('documents over the cap', 'prose_documents_over_cap')], 1.0, None,
     'prose_documents_over_cap'),
    # Temporary: see `bold_backlog`. The pair's reason again, spans and documents on two panels.
    ('bold-backlog.svg', 'Bold spans over 4 per 1,000 words',
     'what would go for every document to meet §213 (writing standards) rule 3; a touched document must already. '
     'Temporary: retired when both panels reach zero',
     [('bold spans over the budget', 'bold_excess_spans')], 1.0, None, 'bold_excess_spans'),
    ('bold-backlog-documents.svg', 'Documents over 4 bold per 1,000 words',
     'the same backlog by where it sits',
     [('documents over the budget', 'bold_documents_over')], 1.0, None, 'bold_documents_over'),
    # Milestone 415 item 3. Stacked by architecture because a count adds up; the largest drift
    # per architecture does not, so it stays in the CSV and on the page's generated line.
    ('baseline-drift.svg', 'Benchmark rows more than 5% above their anchor',
     'icount per iteration since the 2026-09-15 audit\'s anchors, restamp term included; '
     'the tripwire is 10% per save, so this is what it cannot see',
     [(arch, 'baseline_drift_rows_over_5pct_' + arch) for arch in baseline_drift.ARCHES],
     1.0, None, 'baseline_drift_rows_over_5pct_aarch64'),
    # Milestone 610 (provisional). Breaking changes only: a week's additions run to hundreds and
    # would flatten the bars that matter, so they are on the generated line and in the CSV.
    ('interface-stability.svg', 'Breaking changes to the interface',
     'public items removed or changed in the contract and std ABI crates, a crate rename counted '
     'once; rustdoc JSON, week against week',
     [('breaking changes', 'interface_breaking')], 1.0, None, 'interface_breaking'),
]


def fill_derived(rows, rev):
    """Set every column that is read once for all weeks rather than per revision.

    Four kinds of thing land here and they are here for two different reasons.

    `milestones_built_this_week` and `merged_pull_requests` are FLOWS read from today's tree and
    today's log; `built_by_week` has the long argument for why a flow must not be read from each
    week's own revision. Both get a value for every week the CSV holds, a zero included, because a
    week in which nothing was built is a fact and an empty cell would read as "not measured".

    The cost columns are the opposite: they are read from committed records rather than from git,
    and **a week they cannot answer for is left EMPTY, never zeroed**. The first five weeks of this
    project have no session records left anywhere on earth. A zero there would be averaged into a
    published figure by a reader who never opened this file, which milestone 519 names as the one
    failure it exists to prevent.

    `human_person_weeks` is not touched here. It is carried on the row (CARRIED_FIELDS), because a
    hand correction to it has to survive every later rewrite.
    """
    built = built_by_week(Tree(rev))
    merged = merges_by_week(rev)
    models = models_by_week(rev)
    effort = effort_by_week()
    cash = cash_by_week(date.today())
    for week, row in rows.items():
        row['milestones_built_this_week'] = built.get(week, 0)
        row['merged_pull_requests'] = merged.get(week, 0)
        row.update(models.get(week, {}))
        held = effort.get(week) or {}
        tokens, hours = held.get('tokens'), held.get('hours')
        row['lane_tokens'] = '' if tokens is None else tokens
        row['lane_wall_clock_hours'] = '' if hours is None else '%.1f' % hours
        row['price_per_mtok_at_date'] = held.get('blended', '')
        # Absent is absent here too, and for the same reason one line up: the first five weeks of
        # this project have no session records left anywhere, and a zero in a context column would
        # be read as a week of cheap turns rather than as a week nobody can see.
        row['lane_turns'] = held.get('turns') or ''
        share = held.get('read_share')
        row['lane_cache_read_share_pct'] = '' if share is None else '%.1f' % share
        mean = held.get('context_mean')
        row['lane_context_per_turn_mean'] = '' if mean is None else '%d' % mean
        row['lane_context_per_turn_peak'] = held.get('peak') or ''
        # Cash is the one cost column a missing week CAN be zero in, and the difference is that the
        # evidence exists. The ledger is complete by construction: it records every payment, so a
        # week with no row in it is a week nothing was paid, not a week nobody looked.
        row['cash_spend'] = '%.2f' % cash.get(week, 0.0)
    # Money paid in a week the series has no row for is money this column silently drops, and the
    # case is real rather than defensive: calef dates the subscription 2026-07-12, which is a Sunday
    # and therefore 2026W28, while the first commit is 2026-07-13 UTC and the series starts at
    # 2026W29. So $200 sits one week before the first row. A week has a row only if a commit fell in
    # it, and inventing a row with no commit would leave every other column in it empty or wrong,
    # so the amount is reported instead of absorbed into a neighbour. notes/project-metrics.md
    # carries the same arithmetic where a reader meets the number.
    orphaned = {week: amount for week, amount in cash.items() if week not in rows}
    if orphaned:
        print('metrics: $%.2f of ledger spend falls in %s, which the series has no row for; '
              'cash_spend does not include it'
              % (sum(orphaned.values()), ', '.join(sorted(orphaned))), file=sys.stderr)
    # The same silent drop, one column over, and it is the reason the flow does not sum to the
    # stock. Milestone 1 (boot to Rust on QEMU `virt`, and print to the PL011 UART) carries a
    # `**Built:**` date of 2026-07-12, a Sunday, so it lands in 2026W28, and the series starts at
    # 2026W29 because that is where the first commit is. Without this line the series is one short
    # of the tree's own BUILT total and nothing says so. Reported rather than absorbed into 2026W29,
    # for the reason above: a week has a row only if a commit fell in it.
    unbucketed = {week: n for week, n in built.items() if week not in rows}
    if unbucketed:
        print('metrics: %d dated milestone(s) fall in %s, which the series has no row for; '
              'milestones_built_this_week does not include them'
              % (sum(unbucketed.values()), ', '.join(sorted(unbucketed))), file=sys.stderr)
    return rows


def github_anchor(heading):
    """The fragment GitHub gives a heading: lowercased, every character that is not a letter, digit,
    space, hyphen or underscore dropped, and each space turned into a hyphen. Checked on 2026-09-24
    against GitHub's own rendering (`gh api markdown`) of every chart heading on the page, including
    the ones carrying a backtick, a slash, a comma and an apostrophe."""
    kept = ''.join(c for c in heading.lower() if c.isalnum() or c in ' -_')
    return kept.replace(' ', '-')


def page_toc(text):
    """One link per chart panel: a `## ` heading whose section holds an image from the data
    directory. A heading with no chart under it is prose, not a panel, and is left out, so the list
    is exactly what a reader scanning for a chart wants. A repeated heading gets GitHub's `-1`
    suffix, which is why every heading is counted and not only the listed ones."""
    lines, seen, current = [], {}, None
    for line in text.split('\n'):
        if line.startswith('## '):
            title = line[3:].strip()
            base = github_anchor(title)
            n = seen.get(base, 0)
            seen[base] = n + 1
            current = (title, base if n == 0 else '%s-%d' % (base, n))
        elif current and line.startswith('![') and '](project-metrics/' in line:
            lines.append('- [%s](#%s)' % current)
            current = None
    return lines


def render_page_toc(text):
    if text.count(TOC_BEGIN) != 1 or text.count(TOC_END) != 1:
        raise SystemExit('metrics: %s must carry exactly one %r and one %r'
                         % (PAGE, TOC_BEGIN, TOC_END))
    head, rest = text.split(TOC_BEGIN)
    _old, tail = rest.split(TOC_END)
    return head + TOC_BEGIN + '\n' + '\n'.join(page_toc(text)) + '\n' + TOC_END + tail


def write_page_toc():
    path = pathlib.Path(PAGE)
    text = path.read_text()
    new = render_page_toc(text)
    if new != text:
        path.write_text(new)


# Trend lines, keyed by chart. calef asked on 2026-09-24 for a trailing ten-week mean on the
# velocity chart and then the pull-request chart, one helper for both, because a flow that swings
# from 8 to 49 to 13 in three weeks cannot be read for direction by eye. It is drawn and never stored, like the fatal-risks chart's unclassified band:
# it is arithmetic over a column already in the CSV, and a stored copy could disagree with it.
TRENDS = {'velocity.svg': ('milestones_built_this_week', 10),
          'interface-stability.svg': ('interface_breaking', 4),
          'pull-requests.svg': ('merged_pull_requests', 10)}


def trailing_mean(rows, weeks, field, span):
    """(mean, full) per charted week, over the `span` weeks ending there, reaching back past the
    chart's left edge into the CSV. Where the series is younger than `span` the mean is over the
    weeks that exist and `full` is False, which the chart draws dashed rather than hiding: the line
    would otherwise start two bars from the right edge until 2026W47."""
    every = sorted(rows)
    out = []
    for week in weeks:
        k = every.index(week)
        window = every[max(0, k - span + 1):k + 1]
        out.append((sum(_cell(rows[w], field) for w in window) / len(window),
                    len(window) == span))
    return out


def draw_charts(rows):
    write_page_toc()
    write_bold_status(rows)
    write_drift_report()
    write_interface_line(rows)
    retire = bold_retire_line(rows)
    if retire:
        print('metrics: %s (notes/project-metrics.md, "The bold backlog")' % retire)
    weeks = sorted(rows)[-CHART_WEEKS:]
    DATA_DIR.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    for name, title, subtitle, columns, scale, ceiling, absent_field in CHARTS:
        series = series_of(rows, weeks, columns, scale)
        if not series:
            series = [(columns[0][0], [0.0] * len(weeks))]
        absent = None
        if absent_field:
            absent = [rows[week].get(absent_field, '') in ('', None) for week in weeks]
        trend = None
        if name in TRENDS:
            field, span = TRENDS[name]
            trend = ('trailing %d-week mean' % span,
                     'trailing mean, fewer than %d weeks' % span,
                     trailing_mean(rows, weeks, field, span))
        svg_chart(title, subtitle, weeks, series, DATA_DIR / name, ceiling, absent, trend)


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

def print_table(row):
    width = max(len(f) for f in FIELDS)
    for field in FIELDS:
        print('%-*s  %s' % (width, field, row.get(field, '')))


def main(argv):
    coverage = ''
    coverage_min = ''
    coverage_week = ''
    args = []
    i = 0
    while i < len(argv):
        if argv[i] in ('--coverage-from', '--coverage-min-from', '--coverage-for'):
            if i + 1 >= len(argv):
                raise SystemExit('metrics: %s needs an argument' % argv[i])
            if argv[i] == '--coverage-from':
                coverage = coverage_from_lcov(argv[i + 1])
            elif argv[i] == '--coverage-min-from':
                coverage_min = coverage_min_from_summary(argv[i + 1])
            else:
                coverage_week = argv[i + 1]
            i += 2
            continue
        args.append(argv[i])
        i += 1

    if coverage_week:
        # One cell, and only that cell. The row is deliberately NOT recomputed: that would repoint
        # its commit at whatever history says today and restate every count, which is --update's
        # job and is not what a caller measuring one historical week asked for.
        if args:
            raise SystemExit('metrics: --coverage-for takes no mode; it writes one cell and redraws')
        if not coverage and not coverage_min:
            raise SystemExit('metrics: --coverage-for needs --coverage-from <lcov.info> or '
                             '--coverage-min-from <floor.txt>')
        rows = read_csv()
        if coverage_week not in rows:
            raise SystemExit('metrics: %s is not a week in %s; --coverage-for only fills a row '
                             'that already exists' % (coverage_week, measure_path('weeks')))
        # Either cell alone, because they come from different files and a caller may hold only
        # one: a historical week has an lcov and no floor summary, and re-running the floor over an
        # old lcov would apply today's exemptions to it.
        if coverage:
            rows[coverage_week]['coverage_lines_pct'] = coverage
        if coverage_min:
            rows[coverage_week]['coverage_min_file_pct'] = coverage_min
        write_csvs(render_csvs(rows))
        draw_charts(rows)
        print('metrics: %s coverage_lines_pct = %s, coverage_min_file_pct = %s, charts in %s/'
              % (coverage_week, coverage or '(unchanged)', coverage_min or '(unchanged)',
                 DATA_DIR))
        return 0

    mode = args[0] if args else '--table'
    if mode == '--check-toc':
        text = pathlib.Path(PAGE).read_text()
        if render_page_toc(text) != text:
            print('metrics: the table of contents on %s is stale; run script/metrics --charts'
                  % PAGE, file=sys.stderr)
            return 1
        print('metrics: %s lists all %d chart panels' % (PAGE, len(page_toc(text))))
        return 0
    if mode not in ('--table', '--csv', '--backfill', '--update', '--check', '--charts'):
        raise SystemExit('metrics: unknown argument %s; see the comment at the top of this script'
                         % mode)

    if mode == '--charts':
        draw_charts(fill_derived(read_csv(),
                                       git('rev-parse', 'HEAD').decode().strip()))
        print('metrics: charts written to %s/' % DATA_DIR)
        return 0

    head = git('rev-parse', 'HEAD').decode().strip()
    if mode in ('--table', '--csv'):
        row = row_for(head, week_of(git('show', '-s', '--format=%cI', head).decode().strip()),
                      coverage, coverage_min=coverage_min)
        if mode == '--csv':
            print(','.join(FIELDS))
            print(','.join(str(row.get(f, '')) for f in FIELDS))
        else:
            print_table(row)
        return 0

    weeks = week_commits()
    rows = read_csv()
    # CARRIED_FIELDS survive a rewrite, including a `--backfill` that drops every other cell,
    # because nothing in git can reproduce them. Coverage needs an instrumented build at each
    # checkout and nothing here is allowed to build; `human_person_weeks` is a person's statement.
    # Milestone 276 found this the way it is usually found: running `--backfill` to add a column,
    # and watching 2026W36's measured 94.4 disappear with it. It is a named set now rather than one
    # special case, so milestone 519's column inherited the protection instead of the bug.
    kept = {week: {field: row.get(field, '') for field in CARRIED_FIELDS}
            for week, row in rows.items()}
    current = week_of(git('show', '-s', '--format=%cI', head).decode().strip())
    if mode == '--backfill':
        rows = {}
        targets = weeks.items()
    else:
        # The current week, AND every week history has that the CSV does not. This is what closed
        # the 2026W37 gap: the weekly workflow fires at 06:00 UTC on Monday and used to write only
        # the week HEAD falls in, so when a lane merged after midnight (nine did on 2026-09-14, the
        # last eleven minutes before the cron) HEAD was already in the new week and the week that
        # had just finished was never written by anything. A missed or raced run now heals on the
        # next one, and `--check` below fails on a gap rather than passing it every time.
        missing = [w for w in weeks if w not in rows and w != current]
        targets = [(w, weeks[w]) for w in missing] + [(current, weeks.get(current, head))]

    # A fresh lcov describes the tree HEAD is in and nothing else, so it lands on that week only.
    # It used to be `coverage or kept` for every target, which meant `--backfill --coverage-from`
    # stamped one measurement across every week in the series and erased the backfilled cells.
    for week, rev in targets:
        fresh = coverage if week == current else ''
        fresh_min = coverage_min if week == current else ''
        held = kept.get(week, {})
        rows[week] = row_for(rev, week,
                             fresh or held.get('coverage_lines_pct', ''),
                             held.get('human_person_weeks', ''),
                             fresh_min or held.get('coverage_min_file_pct', ''))
        rows[week].update({f: held[f] for f in INTERFACE_FIELDS if held.get(f, '') != ''})

    # Derived last, from HEAD and from the committed cost records, for every week at once: see
    # `fill_derived`. It runs before `--check` compares, so a stale cell is a failure there rather
    # than a surprise on the next write.
    rows = fill_derived(rows, head)

    rendered = render_csvs(rows)
    if mode == '--check':
        # Every measure, and the message names all of the stale ones rather than the first. A
        # caller told one name at a time learns nothing, and `--update` rewrites them all anyway.
        stale = [path for path, text in rendered.items()
                 if (path.read_text() if path.exists() else '') != text]
        if stale:
            print('metrics: %s stale; run script/metrics --update'
                  % ', '.join(str(path) for path in sorted(stale)), file=sys.stderr)
            return 1
        print('metrics: %d measure files in %s/ are current' % (len(rendered), DATA_DIR))
        return 0

    write_csvs(rendered)
    draw_charts(rows)
    print('metrics: %d weeks across %d measure files in %s/, charts in the same directory'
          % (len(rows), len(rendered), DATA_DIR))
    return 0


sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
PYEOF
